window.MODULES.CT561 = {"name":"Systems Modelling & Simulation","evidence":"past-papers","exam":{"summary":["Duration 2 hours (CT561, examiners Dr Enda Howley and Prof Jim Duggan); the paper has 4 questions and the instruction is \"Answer 3 questions out of 4. All questions will be marked equally\".","Every question is worth 25 marks, split into 2-4 lettered parts (5 to 15 marks each), so you are scoring out of 75 and the best three of four count in effect.","The two papers supplied (2024-25 Semester 2, 2025-26 Semester 1) each contain the same four question types, one of each, in a different order and with changed numbers, so the paper is highly predictable.","Almost every part asks you to DO something: draw a stock and flow model, write equations with initial values, trace and label loops (R or B), calculate with Euler or the SIR formulas, or sketch a behaviour over time."],"structure":[{"label":"Type A: graphical integration and Euler","detail":"25 marks = (a) 12: draw the stock and flow model with net flow, calculate net flow, plot the stock by graphical integration and name the behaviour mode of each interval; (b) 5: describe Euler, its assumptions, and when it over- or underestimates, supported by a chosen interval and DT; (c) 8: calculate the stock with Euler at a large DT. Chart is given (sawtooth or ramps)."},{"label":"Type B: feedback and workload backlog","detail":"25 marks = (a) 5-6: define feedback, link polarity, loop polarity, two loop types (and behaviour of a given loop); (b) 12: construct a backlog stock and flow model from a numbered description with link polarities (and loop polarities); (c) 7-8: name three balancing policy loops and the reinforcing side-effect loops, trace each, and discuss the best response."},{"label":"Type C: flow structures, stock management, staffing","detail":"25 marks over 3-4 parts: describe fractional increase / decrease / adjustment to a goal (6-8), a varying decrease fraction (8), or add an expected-enrolments structure (5); explain stock management = anchor + adjustment and the delay that builds the anchor (5-7); build a staffing model with equations (10-12, University staff/students or a three-grade pharmaceutical company); sketch the response to a change in target (5 in the 2025-26 paper, plot with name of key auxiliary in 2024-25)."},{"label":"Type D: disease modelling","detail":"25 marks: force of infection, R0 and herd immunity threshold calculation (5); design an SIR model with quarantine (flag, QF) or with quarantine + vaccination (goal-seeking vaccination fraction) with all equations and feedbacks and attack rate (12-15); cohorts (5) or a sketch of attack rate against QF and vaccination fraction (5)."},{"label":"Question order","detail":"2024-25: Q1 feedback/backlog, Q2 flow structures and staffing, Q3 disease, Q4 integration. 2025-26: Q1 integration, Q2 varying fraction and SIR countermeasures, Q3 feedback/backlog, Q4 expected enrolments, stock management and pharma staffing."}],"freq":[{"topic":"Graphical integration and Euler (net flow, stock plot, over/under, large DT)","count":2,"of":2},{"topic":"Feedback loops: link/loop polarity and backlog policy vs side-effect loops","count":2,"of":2},{"topic":"Stock management structure (anchor + adjustment, delay for the anchor)","count":2,"of":2},{"topic":"Staff recruitment model with equations (University; pharmaceutical)","count":2,"of":2},{"topic":"Fractional increase, decrease, adjustment to a goal (incl. varying fraction)","count":2,"of":2},{"topic":"SIR with quarantine (and vaccination, attack rate, flag)","count":2,"of":2},{"topic":"R0, force of infection, herd immunity threshold calculation","count":1,"of":2},{"topic":"Cohort extension (young/elderly)","count":1,"of":2},{"topic":"Sensitivity sketch: attack rate vs QF and vaccination fraction","count":1,"of":2},{"topic":"Expected enrolments structure","count":1,"of":2}],"years":[{"year":"2024-25 Semester 2","themes":["Q1 (25): (a) feedback loop definition, two types, advantages of feedback (5); (b) assignment backlog stock and flow model with link polarities (12); (c) three balancing and two reinforcing loops, traced (8)","Q2 (25): (a) fractional increase, fractional decrease, adjustment to a goal (6); (b) stock management structure, anchor, adjustment, delay structure for the anchor (7); (c) University staff recruitment model, plot to t = 30, key auxiliary (12)","Q3 (25): (a) force of infection and herd immunity threshold, measles R0 = 12 (5); (b) influenza SIR with quarantine fraction and control flag, explain force of infection (15); (c) cohorts (5)","Q4 (25): (a) sawtooth outflow with two constant inflows: model, net flow, graphical integration (12); (b) Euler, assumptions, when it underestimates, interval 0-5 (5); (c) Euler with DT = 10 (8)"]},{"year":"2025-26 Semester 1","themes":["Q1 (25): (a) ramp inflow with constant outflow 50: model, net flow, graphical integration, stock may go negative (12); (b) Euler, when it overestimates, 0-2.5 with DT = 0.5 (5); (c) Euler with DT = 5 (8)","Q2 (25): (a) fractional decrease with a varying fraction G as adjustment to a goal, real-world use (8); (b) SIR with quarantine stock (20%), vaccinated stock (goal-seeking fraction to 0.15), attack rate, all equations and feedbacks (12); (c) sketch of attack rate against QF and final vaccination fraction with circles (5)","Q3 (25): (a) link polarity, loop polarity, feedback using the Environmental Quality loop, behaviour (6); (b) insurance claims backlog model with link and loop polarities (12); (c) three policy loops and three side-effect loops, best course of action (7)","Q4 (25): (a) expected enrolments structure for Students stock (5); (b) adjustment to a goal extended to stock management, key difference, anchor and adjustment (5); (c) pharmaceutical staff model, Trainee/Experienced/Senior, with all equations and feedbacks (10); (d) sketch and explain response when the target drops from 300 to 100 (5)"]}],"tips":["Pick your three questions in the first 5 minutes: types A (integration) and D/B are the most mechanical; give each about 35 minutes (roughly 1.4 minutes per mark), and do not attempt all four.","Every model answer needs three things: the stock and flow diagram with link polarities, every equation with initial values, and the feedback loops named with their polarity (R or B).","Show numbers: write the formula, substitute, then give the result with units. For Euler questions tabulate t, net flow, stock.","For polarity: trace an INCREASE in the stock around the loop and count the negative links; an even count means reinforcing (R), odd means balancing (B). The exam says \"confirm by tracing\", so write the trace.","In graphical integration, label every time interval with its behaviour mode (for example \"falling at a decreasing rate\") and mark where the net flow crosses zero (stock peak or trough). The question tells you a negative stock is acceptable.","Learn the shared parts word for word: Euler equation and assumptions, over/under rule (rising net = under, falling = over), anchor and adjustment definitions, force of infection explanation.","State assumptions explicitly when the question leaves a value free (adjustment time, quit fraction, time unit of attrition); the examiner marks structure, not your chosen number."],"note":"Evidence: the two problem sheets are the actual 2024-25 and 2025-26 papers (cover pages state 2 hours, answer 3 of 4, equal marks), so frequency counts are out of 2 papers only. Lecture 9 (Infectious Disease Modelling Part 2) supplies the theory for the disease parts: threshold dynamics, Rn, herd immunity, quarantine countermeasures, sensitivity analysis and the SEIR challenge. The system dynamics topics (Euler, feedback, stock management) are built from the papers and standard practice, because those lectures were not supplied."},"hints":null,"code":"CT561","topics":[{"id":"euler-integration","title":"Stocks, net flow, graphical integration and Euler's method","priority":1,"tier":"core","freq":"est. very high (a full 25-mark question on both sheets)","marks":"25 marks (12 + 5 + 8)","summary":"**A stock is the accumulated net flow: Stock(t+DT) = Stock(t) + DT x (inflows - outflows) evaluated at time t (Euler); the stock rises when net flow > 0, falls when < 0, peaks or bottoms where net flow = 0, and its slope at any time equals the net flow.**","mnemonic":"Net flow = SLOPE of the stock; stock = AREA under net flow; Euler = rectangle on the LEFT edge","source":["CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025","CT561_Problem_Sheet_02_2025-2026"],"sections":[{"heading":"Core ideas","items":["**Stock** = accumulation (integral) of its net flow: Stock(t) = Stock(t0) + integral of [inflow - outflow] dt.","**Net flow** = sum of inflows minus sum of outflows; it is the rate of change of the stock: d(Stock)/dt = Net flow.","**Graphical integration**: net flow above zero, the stock rises; below zero, it falls; at zero crossings the stock has a maximum or minimum; the change over an interval is the signed area under the net flow curve.","The **steepness** of the stock equals the size of the net flow; the **curvature** follows whether the net flow is rising (stock accelerating upward / decelerating downward) or falling.","Stock and flow model for a chart question: one stock box, valves for each inflow and outflow, plus an auxiliary variable **Net Flow** = Inflow One + Inflow Two - Outflow (shown by links from the flows)."]},{"heading":"Euler's method (numerical integration)","items":[{"type":"formula","label":"Euler update","text":"Stock(t + DT) = Stock(t) + DT x [Inflow(t) - Outflow(t)]"},"**DT** = time step (size of the interval); Stock(t) = value at the start of the step; Inflow(t), Outflow(t) = flow values at the START of the step.","**Assumption 1**: the net flow is constant over each time step, equal to its value at the start of the step.","**Assumption 2**: DT is small enough that this is a good approximation (smaller DT gives smaller error; DT too large gives large errors and instability).","The error is the difference between the area under the true net flow curve and the rectangle DT x Net(t).","**Underestimates** the true stock change when the net flow is **increasing** over the step (the left-hand rectangle is below the curve); **overestimates** when the net flow is **decreasing** (rectangle above the curve).","Rule of thumb: pick DT at most one quarter to one half of the smallest time constant in the model (the shortest delay or adjustment time). For a goal-seeking flow with adjustment time AT, DT = AT jumps straight to the goal, DT > 2 AT oscillates and diverges.","Other methods exist (Runge-Kutta, trapezoid) which use flow values at more than one point in the step; Euler is the simplest and is what the exam expects."]},{"heading":"Sheet 1 Q4: sawtooth outflow (stock starts at 100)","items":["Flows: Inflow One = 30, Inflow Two = 20 (constant), Outflow sawtooth 100 -> 0 (t = 5) -> 100 (t = 10), repeating every 10 minutes: Outflow = 100 - 20t for 0 <= t <= 5 and 20(t - 5) for 5 <= t <= 10.",{"type":"formula","label":"Net flow","text":"Net(t) = 30 + 20 - Outflow(t) = 50 - Outflow(t): Net = -50 + 20t on [0,5], then 150 - 20t on [5,10]"},"Net flow is -50 at t = 0, crosses zero at t = 2.5, +50 at t = 5, zero at t = 7.5, -50 at t = 10; the pattern repeats (zero crossings at 12.5 and 17.5).","Area of each triangle = 0.5 x 2.5 x 50 = 62.5, so the stock moves 100 -> 37.5 (t = 2.5) -> 100 (t = 5) -> 162.5 (t = 7.5) -> 100 (t = 10), and repeats; **mean outflow = 50 = mean inflow so the stock is periodic, not drifting**.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"line","data":{"title":"Sheet 1 Q4: net flow and stock","xLabel":"Minutes","yLabel":"Units/min and units","series":[{"name":"Net flow","points":[[0,-50],[2.5,0],[5,50],[7.5,0],[10,-50],[12.5,0],[15,50],[17.5,0],[20,-50]]},{"name":"Stock (exact)","points":[[0,100],[1,60],[2,40],[2.5,37.5],[3,40],[4,60],[5,100],[6,140],[7,160],[7.5,162.5],[8,160],[9,140],[10,100],[11,60],[12,40],[12.5,37.5],[13,40],[14,60],[15,100],[16,140],[17,160],[17.5,162.5],[18,160],[19,140],[20,100]]}]},"caption":"Computed exactly: stock has minima 37.5 at t = 2.5, 12.5 and maxima 162.5 at t = 7.5, 17.5."},{"type":"table","head":["Interval (min)","Net flow","Stock behaviour mode"],"rows":[["0 - 2.5","negative, rising from -50 to 0","falls at a decreasing rate (100 to 37.5), reaching its minimum at 2.5"],["2.5 - 5","positive, rising 0 to +50","rises at an increasing rate (37.5 to 100)"],["5 - 7.5","positive, falling +50 to 0","rises at a decreasing rate (100 to 162.5), maximum at 7.5"],["7.5 - 10","negative, falling 0 to -50","falls at an increasing rate (162.5 to 100)"],["10 - 20","repeat of 0 - 10","same cycle again: min 37.5 at 12.5, max 162.5 at 17.5, ends at 100"]]}]},{"heading":"Sheet 1 Q4(b),(c): Euler on the sawtooth","items":["Net flow over 0-5 is increasing from -50 to +50, so Euler **underestimates** the stock. True stock at t = 5 is 100 (area -62.5 + 62.5 = 0).","Euler DT = 5 on 0-5: Stock(5) = 100 + 5 x (-50) = -150: error -250. Euler DT = 1: net values at t = 0,1,2,3,4 are -50, -30, -10, 10, 30 (sum -50), so Stock(5) = 100 - 50 = 50: error -50. Smaller DT shrinks the error but still underestimates.","Part (c), DT = 10: Net(0) = 50 - 100 = -50, Net(10) = 50 - 100 = -50.",{"type":"table","head":["t","Net(t)","Euler stock","True stock"],"rows":[["0","-50","100","100"],["10","-50","100 + 10(-50) = -400","100"],["20","-50","-400 + 10(-50) = -900","100"]]},"DT = 10 equals the full period of the sawtooth, so every step uses the worst-case value -50: the stock heads to -900 while the true stock is periodic around 100. Lesson: DT must be much smaller than the shortest time scale of the flows."]},{"heading":"Sheet 2 Q1: ramp-down, flat, ramp-up inflow with constant outflow 50 (stock starts at 100)","items":["Inflow: 100 -> 0 on [0,5] (100 - 20t), 0 on [5,10], 0 -> 100 on [10,15] (20(t - 10)), 100 on [15,20], 100 -> 0 on [20,25]. Outflow = 50 constant. Net = Inflow - 50.",{"type":"formula","label":"Net flow","text":"Net = 50 - 20t [0,5]; -50 [5,10]; -50 + 20(t - 10) [10,15]; +50 [15,20]; 50 - 20(t - 20) [20,25]"},{"type":"table","head":["Interval (days)","Net flow","Stock behaviour mode","Stock"],"rows":[["0 - 2.5","+50 falling to 0","rises at a decreasing rate to a peak","100 to 162.5"],["2.5 - 5","0 to -50","falls at an increasing rate","162.5 to 100"],["5 - 10","constant -50","falls linearly (constant rate); goes negative at t = 7, allowed by the question","100 to -150"],["10 - 12.5","-50 rising to 0","falls at a decreasing rate to a trough","-150 to -212.5"],["12.5 - 15","0 to +50","rises at an increasing rate","-212.5 to -150"],["15 - 20","constant +50","rises linearly","-150 to 100"],["20 - 22.5","+50 to 0","rises at a decreasing rate to a peak","100 to 162.5"],["22.5 - 25","0 to -50","falls at an increasing rate","162.5 to 100"]]},{"type":"diagram","kind":"line","data":{"title":"Sheet 2 Q1: net flow and exact stock","xLabel":"Days","yLabel":"Units/day and units","series":[{"name":"Net flow","points":[[0,50],[2.5,0],[5,-50],[10,-50],[12.5,0],[15,50],[20,50],[22.5,0],[25,-50]]},{"name":"Stock (exact)","points":[[0,100],[1,140],[2,160],[2.5,162.5],[3,160],[4,140],[5,100],[6,50],[7,0],[8,-50],[9,-100],[10,-150],[11,-190],[12,-210],[12.5,-212.5],[13,-210],[14,-190],[15,-150],[16,-100],[17,-50],[18,0],[19,50],[20,100],[21,140],[22,160],[22.5,162.5],[23,160],[24,140],[25,100]]}]},"caption":"Stock returns to 100 at t = 25 because total inflow area (1250) equals total outflow area (1250)."},"Part (b): over 0-2.5 the net flow is decreasing (50 -> 0), so Euler **overestimates**. With DT = 0.5 the net values at t = 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2 are 50, 40, 30, 20, 10, giving stock 125, 145, 160, 170, 175 at t = 0.5 ... 2.5. True value 162.5, so error +12.5.","Part (c), DT = 5: net at t = 0, 5, 10, 15, 20 is +50, -50, -50, +50, +50.",{"type":"table","head":["t","Net(t)","Euler stock S(t)","True stock"],"rows":[["0","+50","100","100"],["5","-50","100 + 5(50) = 350","100"],["10","-50","350 + 5(-50) = 100","-150"],["15","+50","100 + 5(-50) = -150","-150"],["20","+50","-150 + 5(50) = 100","100"],["25","-","100 + 5(50) = 350","100"]]}]},{"heading":"Answer template: \"describe Euler's method and its assumptions\"","items":["1. Equation and symbols (above). 2. Idea: step through time, using the current flows to predict the next stock value. 3. Assumptions: flows constant over each DT, DT small. 4. When it over/under-estimates (flow decreasing/increasing). 5. Support with a number from the given chart (compare Euler with the exact area)."]}],"pitfalls":["Using the flow at the END of the step instead of the START (that is not Euler).","Forgetting to multiply by DT.","Mixing up over/under: rising net flow means Euler UNDERestimates; falling means OVERestimates.","Reading a chart value wrongly: write the equation of each line segment (slope x t + intercept) and check at the corners.","Describing the stock only as \"up\" or \"down\": examiners want the mode (rising at a decreasing rate, linear, ...) and the turning points.","Forgetting the net flow is also a variable on the stock and flow diagram; draw it with links from every flow."]},{"id":"sir-countermeasures","title":"SIR with countermeasures: quarantine, vaccination, flags, attack rate","priority":2,"tier":"core","freq":"est. very high (Sheet 1 Q3b 15 marks; Sheet 2 Q2b 12 marks; Lecture challenge 10.2)","marks":"12-15 marks","summary":"**Add a Quarantine stock fed by a fraction QF x flag of new infections (quarantined people recover at I/D but do not transmit), a Vaccinated stock fed by S x vaccination fraction (itself goal-seeking to its maximum), and an attack rate variable; all equations and feedback loops must be given.**","mnemonic":"SIQR + V: split the infection flow, drain S by vaccination, count the attack","source":["CT561_Notes_09_Disease_Modelling_2","CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025","CT561_Problem_Sheet_02_2025-2026"],"sections":[{"heading":"Sheet 1 Q3(b): influenza with quarantine and a control flag","items":["Given: R0 = 2, D = 2 days, N = 10,000, I0 = 1, QF = 0.08, quarantine switched by a flag (0 or 1), quarantined recover at the same rate (I/D equivalent Q/D).",{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"SIR with quarantine","height":340,"nodes":[{"id":"S","label":"S","sub":"Susceptible","x":8,"y":25},{"id":"I","label":"I","sub":"Infected","x":50,"y":25,"color":2},{"id":"R","label":"R","sub":"Recovered","x":92,"y":55,"color":1},{"id":"Q","label":"Q","sub":"Quarantined","x":50,"y":85,"color":3}],"edges":[{"from":"S","to":"I","label":"(1 - QF x flag) IR"},{"from":"S","to":"Q","label":"QF x flag x IR","curve":30},{"from":"I","to":"R","label":"I/D"},{"from":"Q","to":"R","label":"Q/D"}]},"caption":"The infection flow IR leaves S and is split between I and Q."},{"type":"table","head":["Variable","Equation","Value / initial"],"rows":[["S","S + DT x (-Infection Rate)","N - I0 = 9999"],["I","I + DT x (Infection to I - Recovery I)","1"],["Q","Q + DT x (Infection to Q - Recovery Q)","0"],["R","R + DT x (Recovery I + Recovery Q)","0"],["R0, D, N, QF, Flag","constants (flag 0 or 1)","2; 2 days; 10000; 0.08; 1"],["Transmission rate beta","R0 / D","1 per day"],["Force of infection lambda","beta x I / N  (only I transmits; Q excluded)","0.0001 at start"],["Infection Rate IR","S x lambda",""],["Effective quarantine fraction","QF x Flag","0.08 (0 if off)"],["Infection to Q","IR x QF x Flag",""],["Infection to I","IR x (1 - QF x Flag)",""],["Recovery I, Recovery Q","I / D ; Q / D",""],["Attack Rate","(N - S) / N = (I + Q + R) / N","rises from 0"]]},"**Explain the force of infection equation**: lambda = beta x I/N: beta = c x i = R0/D is the number of effective contacts per infectious person per day; I/N is the probability that a random contact is with an infectious person; so lambda is the per-day probability that one susceptible becomes infected. Multiplying by S gives the number of new infections per day. Quarantined people are excluded from I, so they cannot infect, which is how quarantine reduces transmission.","**Feedback loops**: Contagion (R): I, IR, I (positive). Depletion (B): S, IR, S. Recovery (B): I, Recovery I, I. Quarantine recovery (B): Q, Recovery Q, Q. Quarantine drains infections from the contagion loop: effective R0 = R0 (1 - QF) = 2 x 0.92 = 1.84, so QF = 0.08 only mildly helps.","**Interpretation note**: Sheet 1 says \"a proportion of those infected will quarantine\"; the lecture (challenge 10.2) says \"when people become infected, a certain fraction quarantine\", so the standard answer splits the infection flow as above. If you instead read it as people leaving I, use a flow I to Q = I x detection fraction (per day) and I to R = (I/D); the quarantined still must not infect. State the interpretation you use. Quarantine acts on d (time circulating) in the lecture policy table, which is consistent with both readings.","Result (simulated, DT 0.01): QF 0 gives peak I about 1537 and attack rate 79.7%; QF = 0.08 gives peak about 1153 (at day 20.8) and attack rate 74.7%.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"line","data":{"title":"Infected over time: no quarantine vs QF = 0.08","xLabel":"Days","yLabel":"Infected (I)","series":[{"name":"QF = 0","points":[[0,1],[2,3],[4,7],[6,20],[8,53],[10,139],[12,343],[14,743],[16,1262],[18,1535],[20,1370],[22,990],[24,633],[26,378],[28,218],[30,123],[32,69],[34,38],[36,21],[38,12],[40,6],[42,4],[44,2],[46,1],[48,1],[50,0]]},{"name":"QF = 0.08","points":[[0,1],[2,2],[4,5],[6,12],[8,28],[10,64],[12,142],[14,298],[16,565],[18,900],[20,1129],[22,1111],[24,900],[26,640],[28,420],[30,263],[32,160],[34,96],[36,57],[38,34],[40,20],[42,12],[44,7],[46,4],[48,2],[50,1]]}]}}]},{"heading":"Sheet 2 Q2(b): quarantine + vaccination (goal-seeking vaccination fraction)","items":["Given: 20% of infection flow goes to quarantine, 80% to I (QF = 0.2); quarantined recover in 2 days and do not infect; vaccination removes **only susceptibles** via a fractional decrease flow S x VF; VF starts at 0 and goal-seeks 0.15; R0 = 2, D = 2, N = 10,000, I0 = 1; include attack rate.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"SIR + quarantine + vaccination","height":380,"nodes":[{"id":"S","label":"S","sub":"Susceptible","x":8,"y":25},{"id":"I","label":"I","sub":"Infected","x":50,"y":25,"color":2},{"id":"R","label":"R","sub":"Recovered","x":92,"y":55,"color":1},{"id":"Q","label":"Q","sub":"Quarantined","x":50,"y":85,"color":3},{"id":"V","label":"V","sub":"Vaccinated","x":8,"y":85,"color":4}],"edges":[{"from":"S","to":"I","label":"0.8 IR"},{"from":"S","to":"Q","label":"0.2 IR","curve":30},{"from":"I","to":"R","label":"I/D"},{"from":"Q","to":"R","label":"Q/D"},{"from":"S","to":"V","label":"S x VF"}]}},{"type":"table","head":["Variable","Equation","Initial"],"rows":[["S","S + DT x (-IR - Vaccination)","9999"],["I","I + DT x (0.8 IR - I/D)  [(1 - QF) IR - Recovery I]","1"],["Q","Q + DT x (QF x IR - Q/D)","0"],["R","R + DT x (I/D + Q/D)","0"],["V","V + DT x Vaccination","0"],["VF (stock)","VF + DT x (VF Max - VF)/AT_v  (goal seeking); AT_v e.g. 10 days","0"],["VF Max","constant","0.15 per day"],["Vaccination flow","S x VF",""],["beta; lambda; IR","R0/D = 1; beta x I/N; S x lambda","quarantined excluded from lambda"],["Attack Rate","(N - S - V) / N = proportion ever infected","0"]]},"Feedbacks: contagion (R), depletion (B; S falls by both infection and vaccination, making it stronger), recovery (B), quarantine recovery (B), **vaccination (B)**: S, Vaccination, S; **VF goal seeking (B)**: VF, VF adjustment, VF; and indirectly the strengthening of depletion. Vaccination lowers Rn by shrinking S/N.","Check: S + I + Q + R + V = N. Vaccination pushes Rn = R0 (1 - QF) S/N below 1 early, so the epidemic is small: with QF = 0.2, VF Max = 0.15, AT_v = 10 the simulation gives peak I about 5 and attack rate about 0.4%, against attack rate 64.2% with QF = 0.2 and no vaccination.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"line","data":{"title":"Infected with QF = 0.2: without and with vaccination (VF max 0.15)","xLabel":"Days","yLabel":"Infected","series":[{"name":"No vaccination","points":[[0,1],[2,2],[4,3],[6,6],[8,11],[10,20],[12,35],[14,63],[16,109],[18,184],[20,292],[22,428],[24,559],[26,641],[28,641],[30,569],[32,460],[34,346],[36,249],[38,173],[40,117],[42,79],[44,52],[46,34],[48,23],[50,15]]},{"name":"VF max 0.15","points":[[0,1],[2,2],[4,3],[6,4],[8,5],[10,5],[12,4],[14,3],[16,2],[18,1],[20,1],[22,0],[24,0],[26,0],[28,0],[30,0]]}]}}]},{"heading":"Sheet 2 Q2(c): attack rate vs quarantine fraction and final vaccination fraction","items":["Sketch a 4 x 4 grid: x-axis QF = 0, 0.05, 0.10, 0.15; y-axis final VF = 0, 0.05, 0.10, 0.15; circle size = final attack rate (proportion ever infected). Computed values (R0 = 2, D = 2, N = 10,000, I0 = 1, AT_v = 10 days):",{"type":"table","head":["QF \\ final VF","0","0.05","0.10","0.15"],"rows":[["0","0.797","0.207","0.040","0.016"],["0.05","0.768","0.135","0.026","0.011"],["0.10","0.733","0.080","0.016","0.008"],["0.15","0.691","0.044","0.010","0.005"]]},{"type":"diagram","kind":"scatter","data":{"title":"Sketch: final attack rate (%) at each QF and final VF","xLabel":"Quarantine fraction QF","yLabel":"Final vaccination fraction VF","xMin":-0.02,"xMax":0.17,"yMin":-0.02,"yMax":0.17,"series":[{"name":"Grid points","points":[[0,0],[0.05,0],[0.1,0],[0.15,0],[0,0.05],[0.05,0.05],[0.1,0.05],[0.15,0.05],[0,0.1],[0.05,0.1],[0.1,0.1],[0.15,0.1],[0,0.15],[0.05,0.15],[0.1,0.15],[0.15,0.15]]}],"points":[{"x":0,"y":0,"label":"80"},{"x":0.05,"y":0,"label":"77"},{"x":0.1,"y":0,"label":"73"},{"x":0.15,"y":0,"label":"69"},{"x":0,"y":0.05,"label":"21"},{"x":0.05,"y":0.05,"label":"14"},{"x":0.1,"y":0.05,"label":"8"},{"x":0.15,"y":0.05,"label":"4"},{"x":0,"y":0.1,"label":"4"},{"x":0.05,"y":0.1,"label":"3"},{"x":0.1,"y":0.1,"label":"2"},{"x":0.15,"y":0.1,"label":"1"},{"x":0,"y":0.15,"label":"1.6"},{"x":0.05,"y":0.15,"label":"1.1"},{"x":0.1,"y":0.15,"label":"0.8"},{"x":0.15,"y":0.15,"label":"0.5"}]},"caption":"Labels are the computed final attack rates in %; in your sketch draw the circle area proportional to this value: huge at the bottom-left corner, tiny along the top row."},"Explanation: circles are largest at bottom-left (no countermeasures, 80%) and shrink towards top-right. Vaccination is by far the stronger lever: it removes susceptibles (reducing Rn through S/N and depleting the pool before the epidemic grows). Quarantine only lowers the effective R0 to R0 (1 - QF) (at most 1.7), so alone it changes the attack rate only from 80% to 69%.","The effects combine (synergy): the same QF change matters most at intermediate vaccination (VF 0.05: 21% down to 4%), because together they push Rn below 1 early. At high VF the attack rate is near zero regardless of QF. Exact numbers depend on the vaccination adjustment time; the pattern does not."]}],"pitfalls":["Letting quarantined people contribute to the force of infection (they do not transmit).","Moving people to quarantine from the Infected stock rather than splitting the infection flow (read the question: Sheet 1 says a proportion of those infected quarantine, Sheet 2 says the infection flow is split; either must be stated clearly).","Forgetting the flag: effective fraction = QF x Flag.","Vaccinating the whole population (only susceptibles move to V).","Not including the attack rate equation or the initial values.","Omitting the feedback loops (explicitly requested)."]},{"id":"stock-management","title":"Stock management structure: anchor + adjustment","priority":3,"tier":"core","freq":"est. very high (Sheet 1 Q2b, Sheet 2 Q4b)","marks":"5-7 marks (plus model parts)","summary":"**The stock management structure extends adjustment to a goal: Inflow = MAX(0, Anchor + Adjustment), where the anchor is the expected outflow (keeps the stock level when on target) and the adjustment is (Desired Stock - Stock)/AT (closes the gap).**","mnemonic":"ANCHOR holds the level, ADJUSTMENT closes the gap","source":["CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025","CT561_Problem_Sheet_02_2025-2026"],"sections":[{"heading":"From adjustment to a goal to stock management","items":["**Adjustment to a goal**: Net flow = (Goal - Stock)/AT. Fine when there is no other outflow: the stock reaches the goal.","**Problem**: if the stock also has a drain (staff quits, inventory sales), the goal-seeking inflow alone equals the outflow only when the stock is below the goal: the stock settles **below** the goal with a steady-state error (where (Goal - S)/AT = outflow).","**Fix**: add an **anchor** equal to the (expected) outflow so that at the goal the inflow exactly replaces the outflow. This is the stock management structure.",{"type":"formula","label":"Stock management structure","text":"Inflow (Hire Rate) = MAX(0, Anchor + Adjustment)"},{"type":"formula","label":"Adjustment","text":"Adjustment = (Desired Stock - Stock) / AT,  AT = adjustment time"},{"type":"formula","label":"Anchor","text":"Anchor = Expected Outflow (smoothed outflow); in steady state Anchor = Outflow"},"**(1) The anchor**: the baseline flow that would hold the stock constant: it replaces what is lost. Equal to the outflow (quits) as perceived by the manager, usually smoothed.","**(2) The adjustment**: the correction to close the discrepancy between desired and actual stock, spread over the adjustment time AT. Positive when stock is below target (hire extra), negative when above.","**Key difference from adjustment to a goal**: stock management has an extra term, the anchor (outflow replacement), so the stock converges to the goal even though there is a continuing outflow; adjustment to a goal alone has no anchor.","**MAX(0, ...)**: an inflow cannot be negative (you cannot un-hire); when the target drops a long way, the hire rate sits at zero until attrition brings the stock down."]},{"heading":"The delay structure that builds the anchor","items":["Managers do not respond instantly to the outflow: the anchor is an **expected outflow**, a **first-order information delay (exponential smoothing)**.","Structure: a stock **Expected Outflow** (for example Expected Quits) whose only inflow is the **Change in Expected Outflow** = (Actual Outflow - Expected Outflow)/Time to Adjust (smoothing time). Expected Outflow starts at the initial outflow.",{"type":"formula","label":"Anchor delay structure","text":"Expected Quits(t + DT) = Expected Quits(t) + DT x (Quits - Expected Quits)/Time to Adjust Expectation"},"This is an adjustment-to-goal structure where the goal is the actual outflow. It adds a balancing loop around Expected Quits.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"Stock management structure (staff)","height":420,"nodes":[{"id":"hire","label":"Hire Rate","x":10,"y":40},{"id":"staff","label":"Staff","x":45,"y":40,"color":1},{"id":"quits","label":"Quits","x":85,"y":40},{"id":"adj","label":"Adjustment","x":10,"y":90},{"id":"des","label":"Desired Staff","x":45,"y":95,"color":2},{"id":"anch","label":"Expected Quits (anchor)","x":50,"y":5,"color":3},{"id":"at","label":"AT","x":25,"y":98}],"edges":[{"from":"hire","to":"staff","label":"+"},{"from":"staff","to":"quits","label":"+"},{"from":"quits","to":"staff","label":"-","curve":25},{"from":"staff","to":"adj","label":"-"},{"from":"des","to":"adj","label":"+"},{"from":"at","to":"adj","label":"-"},{"from":"adj","to":"hire","label":"+"},{"from":"quits","to":"anch","label":"+","curve":15},{"from":"anch","to":"hire","label":"+"}]},"caption":"Loops: B1 adjustment (Staff, Adjustment, Hire Rate, Staff: one negative); B2 attrition (Staff, Quits, Staff); R3 anchor (Staff, Quits, Expected Quits, Hire Rate, Staff: all positive, but it only replaces losses)."}]},{"heading":"Behaviour","items":["At t = 0 with Staff = Desired Staff and Expected Quits = Quits: adjustment 0, Hire = Quits, stock constant (equilibrium).","If desired staff rises: hire = anchor + (gap)/AT, a jump above the replacement level, then the gap closes exponentially: **goal-seeking growth to the new target**.","Short AT: fast response, larger initial hiring spike, risk of overshoot with extra delays. Long AT: smooth but slow. **AT is the main parameter** controlling how quickly the model resets.","The anchor smoothing time has a smaller effect: it only matters while quits are changing.","Same structure applies to inventory (Production = expected sales + adjustment for inventory gap), capacity, and workforce models."]}],"pitfalls":["Omitting the anchor and calling it stock management: the answer needs both terms.","Forgetting MAX(0, ...) on the flow.","Not showing the delay (smoothing) structure when asked for how the anchor is built.","Describing the anchor as \"the goal\": the goal is the Desired Stock; the anchor is the expected outflow.","Forgetting to initialise Expected Quits at the equilibrium value (otherwise the model does not start in steady state)."]},{"id":"staff-recruitment-models","title":"Staff recruitment models (University and pharmaceutical)","priority":4,"tier":"core","freq":"est. very high (Sheet 1 Q2c 12 marks; Sheet 2 Q4c,d 15 marks)","marks":"10-15 marks","summary":"**Model staff as a stock with Hire = MAX(0, Expected Quits + (Desired - Staff)/AT) and Quits = Staff x fraction; desired staff from a driver (students / ratio, or a target); start in equilibrium, then trace the response to a change in the target.**","mnemonic":"Desired from the driver; Hire = Anchor + Adjustment; start balanced","source":["CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025","CT561_Problem_Sheet_02_2025-2026"],"sections":[{"heading":"Sheet 1 Q2c: University staff and students","items":["Given: Staff = 500 (equilibrium), Students = 10,000 (equilibrium), desired student/staff ratio 20, drops to 10 at t = 10; run to t = 30.","Chosen free parameters (state them): quit fraction 0.1 per time unit, AT = 5, smoothing time for expected quits 2, recruitment fraction = graduation fraction = 0.25 (students in equilibrium: 2500 in, 2500 out).",{"type":"table","head":["Element","Equation","Initial value"],"rows":[["Students (stock)","Students(t+DT) = Students + DT x (Recruitment - Graduation)","10000"],["Recruitment","Students x Recruitment Fraction","2500 (fraction 0.25)"],["Graduation","Students x Graduation Fraction","2500 (fraction 0.25)"],["Desired Staff","Students / Desired Student Staff Ratio","10000/20 = 500"],["Desired Student Staff Ratio","20 for t < 10; 10 for t >= 10 (STEP of -10 at t = 10)","20"],["Staff (stock)","Staff(t+DT) = Staff + DT x (Hire Rate - Quits)","500"],["Quits","Staff x Quit Fraction","50 (fraction 0.1)"],["Expected Quits (anchor stock)","ExpQ(t+DT) = ExpQ + DT x (Quits - ExpQ)/Smoothing Time","50"],["Adjustment","(Desired Staff - Staff)/AT","0"],["Hire Rate","MAX(0, Expected Quits + Adjustment)","50"]]},"Feedback loops: **B1 staff adjustment** (Staff, Adjustment, Hire, Staff; one negative); **B2 quits** (Staff, Quits, Staff; negative); **R3 anchor** (Staff, Quits, Expected Quits, Hire, Staff; all positive, but it is the replacement mechanism and is dominated by B2). Students has its own two flows (balancing loops via the fractions).","**Plot (answer 1)**: flat at 500 until t = 10. At t = 10 desired staff jumps to 1000 so hire rate jumps from 50 to 150 (anchor 50 + 500/5). Staff then rises with decreasing slope, exponential goal seeking towards 1000: about 656 at t = 12, 754 at 14, 870 at 18, 905 at 20, 950 at 24, 980 at 30 (not quite 1000 yet). Hire rate falls from 150 towards the replacement level 100 (quits at 1000 staff).",{"type":"diagram","kind":"line","data":{"title":"Staff after the ratio drops from 20 to 10 at t = 10","xLabel":"Time","yLabel":"Staff","yMin":400,"yMax":1050,"series":[{"name":"AT = 2","points":[[0,500],[10,500],[11,694.4],[12,803.9],[13,869.3],[14,910.5],[16,955.9],[18,977.5],[20,988.4],[22,994],[24,996.9],[26,998.4],[28,999.2],[30,999.6]]},{"name":"AT = 5","points":[[0,500],[10,500],[11,588.3],[12,656],[13,710],[14,754.1],[16,821.7],[18,870],[20,905.1],[22,930.6],[24,949.3],[26,962.9],[28,972.9],[30,980.2]]},{"name":"AT = 10","points":[[0,500],[10,500],[11,546.1],[12,585.2],[13,619.5],[14,650.2],[16,703.4],[18,748],[20,785.8],[22,817.9],[24,845.2],[26,868.4],[28,888.1],[30,904.9]]}]},"caption":"Euler simulation, DT = 0.125. Answer 2: the **adjustment time AT** controls how fast the model resets."},"**Answer 2**: the auxiliary with the big influence is the **Adjustment Time (AT)**: small AT, fast response and larger hire spike; large AT, slow response. The quit fraction also sets the replacement hiring level."]},{"heading":"Sheet 2 Q4c: pharmaceutical company (Trainee, Experienced, Senior)","items":["Stocks: Trainee, Experienced, Senior (100 each). Flows: Hire Rate into Trainee; Progression TE = Trainee/12; Progression ES = Experienced/36; Attrition per stock: Trainee x 0.05, Experienced x 0.03, Senior x 0.01 (assumed per month, the time unit of the delays).",{"type":"table","head":["Variable","Equation","Initial"],"rows":[["Trainee","Trainee + DT x (Hire Rate - Progression TE - Attrition T)","100"],["Experienced","Experienced + DT x (Progression TE - Progression ES - Attrition E)","100"],["Senior","Senior + DT x (Progression ES - Attrition S)","100"],["Progression TE / ES","Trainee / 12 ; Experienced / 36","8.33 ; 2.78"],["Attrition T / E / S","0.05 x Trainee ; 0.03 x Experienced ; 0.01 x Senior","5 ; 3 ; 1"],["Total Employees","Trainee + Experienced + Senior","300"],["Target Employees","constant","300"],["Total Attrition","sum of three attrition flows","9"],["Expected Attrition (anchor stock)","ExpA + DT x (Total Attrition - ExpA)/Smoothing Time (assume 3)","9"],["Adjustment","(Target - Total Employees)/AT (assume AT = 6)","0"],["Hire Rate","MAX(0, Expected Attrition + Adjustment)","9"]]},"Feedbacks: B1 adjustment (Total, Adjustment, Hire, Trainee, Experienced, Senior, Total: one negative link, balancing), B2/B3/B4 attrition loops at each stock (stock, attrition, stock: balancing), B5 progression (Trainee to Experienced: fractional decrease), R anchor loop (Total, Attrition, Expected Attrition, Hire, Total).","**Caution (honest note)**: with these progression delays and attrition rates, 100 in each category is not an exact equilibrium for each stock (Experienced inflow 8.33 vs outflow 2.78 + 3 = 5.78). The **total** is held at 300 because hire = attrition and adjustment = 0; the split between categories drifts (simulation: Trainees fall, Seniors rise). State this assumption.","**Sheet 2 Q4d, target drops to 100**: Adjustment = (100 - 300)/6 = -33.3 per month. Hire Rate = MAX(0, 9 - 33.3) = **0**: hiring stops. Total falls only through attrition (and the anchor falls as attrition falls) so the decline is gradual: total about 255 at month 6, 198 at month 18, 148 at month 36, 111 at month 60, 102 at month 72. Hiring restarts (anchor plus a small negative adjustment becomes positive) at about month 62, and the total settles at 100.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"line","data":{"title":"Total employees after the target drops to 100 (AT = 6 months)","xLabel":"Months","yLabel":"Total employees","series":[{"name":"Total","points":[[0,300],[6,254.7],[12,222],[18,197.3],[24,177.8],[30,162],[36,148.8],[42,137.5],[48,127.6],[54,118.9],[60,111.1],[66,104.6],[72,101.7],[78,100.4],[84,99.9],[96,99.7],[120,99.8]]}]},"caption":"Euler simulation DT = 0.05 months. Because you cannot fire staff the hire rate is clipped at zero by MAX(0, ...) and the total declines at the speed of attrition."},"How the equations react: the adjustment term turns strongly negative, MAX clips Hire Rate to 0, the Trainee stock empties (no new entrants), attrition shrinks as stocks shrink, so Expected Attrition falls and the negative adjustment shrinks; once Anchor + Adjustment > 0 hiring resumes at the new replacement level, and the total converges on 100 (goal seeking)."]}],"pitfalls":["Not starting in equilibrium (state initial Expected Quits = Quits).","Letting hire go negative (no MAX).","Forgetting desired staff is a function of students and the ratio (a driver), not a constant.","For the sketch: staff must flatten smoothly towards the target, not jump.","Quoting a speed without naming the adjustment time as the cause."]},{"id":"workload-backlog-models","title":"Workload / backlog models: policy loops and side-effect loops","priority":5,"tier":"core","freq":"est. very high (Sheet 1 Q1b,c; Sheet 2 Q3b,c: 12 + 7-8 marks)","marks":"19-20 marks","summary":"**A backlog stock with Work Pressure drives balancing policy loops (work harder, cut time per task, extend deadline, add staff) whose side effects (rework, fatigue, lower efficiency) create reinforcing loops that undermine them.**","mnemonic":"Pressure triggers POLICIES (B loops); policies leak SIDE EFFECTS (R loops)","source":["CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025","CT561_Problem_Sheet_02_2025-2026"],"sections":[{"heading":"Generic structure","items":["Stock: Backlog. Inflows: new work (Allocated / New Claims) and Rework. Outflow: Completed.","Backlog to Work Pressure (+). Work Pressure drives one or more policies (all aimed at raising Completed or reducing pressure).","**Policy loops** are balancing: backlog rises, pressure rises, policy acts, completion rises, backlog falls.","**Side-effect loops** are reinforcing: the policy also raises rework or fatigue, which pushes the backlog up again, reducing the policy's benefit.","Delays (hiring, training, fatigue building up) make the effects of policies late, and can cause oscillation and over-reaction."]},{"heading":"Sheet 1 Q1: Assignment Backlog (student workload)","items":["Links with polarity: Allocated to Backlog +; Rework to Backlog +; Completed to Backlog -; Backlog to Work Pressure +; Work Pressure to Time Per Assignment -; Work Pressure to Weekly Study Hours +; Work Pressure to Amount of Time to Submit +; Time Per Assignment to Rework -; Time Per Assignment to Completed -; Weekly Study Hours to Completed +; Weekly Study Hours to Fatigue +; Amount of Time to Submit to Work Pressure -; Fatigue to Completed -.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"Assignment backlog model (link polarities)","height":520,"nodes":[{"id":"alloc","label":"Allocated","x":5,"y":5},{"id":"rew","label":"Rework","x":5,"y":40},{"id":"bl","label":"Assignment Backlog","x":40,"y":20,"color":1},{"id":"comp","label":"Completed","x":80,"y":40},{"id":"wp","label":"Work Pressure","x":40,"y":50,"color":2},{"id":"tpa","label":"Time Per Assignment","x":10,"y":80},{"id":"swh","label":"Weekly Study Hours","x":55,"y":85},{"id":"tts","label":"Amount of Time to Submit","x":85,"y":65},{"id":"fat","label":"Fatigue","x":90,"y":90}],"edges":[{"from":"alloc","to":"bl","label":"+"},{"from":"rew","to":"bl","label":"+"},{"from":"comp","to":"bl","label":"-"},{"from":"bl","to":"wp","label":"+"},{"from":"wp","to":"tpa","label":"-"},{"from":"wp","to":"swh","label":"+"},{"from":"wp","to":"tts","label":"+"},{"from":"tpa","to":"rew","label":"-"},{"from":"tpa","to":"comp","label":"-","curve":20},{"from":"swh","to":"comp","label":"+"},{"from":"swh","to":"fat","label":"+"},{"from":"tts","to":"wp","label":"-","curve":20},{"from":"fat","to":"comp","label":"-","curve":-20}]},"caption":"Flows drawn as nodes for the causal view; in the stock and flow diagram Allocated, Rework (inflows) and Completed (outflow) are valves on the Backlog stock."},{"type":"table","head":["Loop","Path","Negatives","Type"],"rows":[["B1 Work harder","Backlog +, Work Pressure +, Weekly Study Hours +, Completed -, Backlog","1","Balancing"],["B2 Cut time per assignment","Backlog +, Work Pressure -, Time Per Assignment -, Completed -, Backlog","3","Balancing"],["B3 Deadline extension","Work Pressure +, Amount of Time to Submit -, Work Pressure","1","Balancing"],["R1 Rework (side effect of B2)","Backlog +, Work Pressure -, Time Per Assignment -, Rework +, Backlog","2","Reinforcing"],["R2 Fatigue (side effect of B1)","Backlog +, Work Pressure +, Weekly Study Hours +, Fatigue -, Completed -, Backlog","2","Reinforcing"]]},"Trace, increasing backlog: B1: backlog up, pressure up, hours up, completed up, backlog down (balancing). B2: backlog up, pressure up, time per assignment DOWN, completed UP, backlog down (balancing). B3: pressure up, time to submit up, pressure down (balancing). R1: backlog up, pressure up, time per assignment down, rework UP, backlog UP (reinforcing). R2: backlog up, pressure up, hours up, fatigue up, completed DOWN, backlog UP (reinforcing)."]},{"heading":"Sheet 2 Q3: Claims Backlog (insurance)","items":["Links: New Claims +, Reworked Claims + into Backlog; Claims Completed - from Backlog; Backlog to Work Pressure +; Work Pressure to Time per Claim -; Work Pressure to Temporary Staff +; Work Pressure to Workweek +; Time per Claim to Claims Completed -; Temporary Staff to Claims Completed +; Workweek to Claims Completed +; Time per Claim to Reworked Claims -; Temporary Staff to Time per Claim +; Workweek to Fatigue +; Fatigue to Claims Completed -.",{"type":"table","head":["Loop","Path (start at Backlog)","Negatives","Type"],"rows":[["Policy B1: reduce time per claim","Work Pressure +, Time per Claim -, Completed -, Backlog","3","Balancing"],["Policy B2: temporary staff","Work Pressure +, Temp Staff +, Completed +, Backlog -","1","Balancing"],["Policy B3: longer workweek","Work Pressure +, Workweek +, Completed +, Backlog -","1","Balancing"],["Side effect R1: rework","Work Pressure +, Time per Claim -, Reworked Claims -, Backlog +","2","Reinforcing"],["Side effect R2: temp staff slow time per claim","Work Pressure +, Temp Staff +, Time per Claim +, Completed -, Backlog -","2","Reinforcing"],["Side effect R3: fatigue","Work Pressure +, Workweek +, Fatigue +, Completed -, Backlog -","2","Reinforcing"]]},"Note: the Temp Staff, Time per Claim, Reworked Claims path (Time per Claim up gives fewer reworks, Reworked Claims - into Backlog) is an additional balancing side-benefit loop; the three side-effect loops that undermine the policies are R1, R2, R3.","In the stock and flow model the claims flows are valves on the Backlog stock; polarity of flow to stock is + (inflow) and - (outflow)."]},{"heading":"Discussion: best response to a workload increase (Sheet 2 Q3c)","items":["**Reduce time per claim**: fast but quality drops, rework grows (R1), possibly a net loss; avoid except as a short-term measure.","**Longer workweek**: immediate effect but fatigue accumulates and productivity falls (R3), also costs overtime; use only for short peaks.","**Temporary staff**: adds capacity without hurting quality much; side effect is training/induction slowing average time per claim (R2) and a hiring delay, but it is the most sustainable.","Recommended: combine **temporary staff as the main response** (structural capacity), a limited temporary workweek increase to cover the delay, and avoid cutting time per claim; monitor rework and fatigue as leading indicators; also reduce the inflow if possible.","Tie to feedback: policies are balancing loops but each side effect is a reinforcing loop; the best policy has the weakest, slowest side-effect loop."]}],"pitfalls":["Mislabelling loops: Time Per Assignment is reduced by pressure (negative link) and reduces completion time (a negative link to Completed), so the policy loop has THREE negatives.","Drawing the Time to Submit loop through the stock: it only involves Work Pressure and Time to Submit.","Not showing link polarities and loop labels (R/B) on the diagram (explicit marks in the question).","Giving loop names without tracing: show the trace as the question asks.","Forgetting the flow into the stock: Completed is an outflow (negative), Rework an inflow (positive)."]},{"id":"feedback-polarity","title":"Feedback, link polarity and loop polarity","priority":6,"tier":"core","freq":"est. high (Sheet 1 Q1a, Q1c; Sheet 2 Q3a, Q3c)","marks":"5-8 marks","summary":"**A feedback loop is a closed chain of causal links where a variable eventually influences itself; link polarity (+ same direction, - opposite) decides the loop type: an even number of negative links is reinforcing (R, growth/collapse), an odd number is balancing (B, goal seeking).**","mnemonic":"Count the minuses: even = Reinforcing, odd = Balancing","source":["CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025","CT561_Problem_Sheet_02_2025-2026","CT561_Notes_09_Disease_Modelling_2"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions","items":["**Feedback**: causality that forms a closed loop; a change in a variable travels round the chain and comes back to change the variable itself.","**Link polarity**: sign on a causal link A to B. **Positive (+)**: if A increases, B increases above what it would otherwise have been (and if A decreases, B decreases). **Negative (-)**: if A increases, B decreases (opposite direction).","**Loop polarity**: the type of loop obtained by multiplying the link signs, i.e. counting negative links. **Even number (including zero) of negatives: reinforcing (R)**. **Odd number: balancing (B)**.","Stock-flow links: inflow to stock is +, outflow to stock is -. A flow that drains the stock gives a negative link.","**Reinforcing loop**: amplifies change (exponential growth or collapse, vicious or virtuous circle); in the SIR model, the Contagion loop.","**Balancing loop**: counteracts change and seeks a goal or equilibrium; in SIR the Depletion loop and the Recovery loop."]},{"heading":"Behaviour","items":["R loop alone: exponential growth (or decay if it starts falling). B loop alone: goal-seeking (exponential approach); with a delay in the loop it can **oscillate** around the goal.","Real systems have both; the behaviour depends on which loop dominates, and dominance shifts over time (shifting loop dominance, for example an epidemic: R dominates early, B dominates after the peak).","**Advantages of adding feedback structures to a stock and flow model**: the model generates behaviour endogenously instead of by imposed inputs; captures the policy response (managers react to a gap); shows why systems resist change or grow; allows testing policies; reveals unintended side effects; explains oscillation, overshoot and goal seeking."]},{"heading":"Procedure to find loop polarity (use in every question)","items":["1. Write the loop as a cycle of variables starting at the stock. 2. Put a sign on every link. 3. Trace an increase of the stock around the loop and see whether it comes back as an increase (reinforcing) or a decrease (balancing). 4. Cross-check by counting negatives. 5. Name the loop and mark it R or B on the diagram."]},{"heading":"Worked example: Sheet 2 Q3(a) environment loop","items":["Loop: Environmental Quality to Pressure to Clean Up Environment to Cleanup Effort back to Environmental Quality.","Signs: Environmental Quality to Pressure is **negative** (the better the quality, the less the pressure); Pressure to Cleanup Effort is **positive**; Cleanup Effort to Environmental Quality is **positive**.","One negative link (odd), so the loop is **balancing**. Trace: quality falls, pressure rises, effort rises, quality rises again: it corrects the change.","Behaviour: goal seeking: quality is pulled towards the level where pressure is zero (the goal). With delays in effort or environment response the loop would overshoot and oscillate around the goal; with no delay it approaches smoothly.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"Environment balancing loop (B)","directed":true,"height":280,"nodes":[{"id":"p","label":"Pressure to Clean Up","x":50,"y":5},{"id":"e","label":"Cleanup Effort","x":90,"y":85},{"id":"q","label":"Environmental Quality","x":10,"y":85}],"edges":[{"from":"q","to":"p","label":"-","curve":-25},{"from":"p","to":"e","label":"+","curve":-25},{"from":"e","to":"q","label":"+","curve":-25}]},"caption":"One negative link: balancing."}]}],"pitfalls":["Defining polarity of a link as \"good/bad\": it is about direction of change, not desirability.","Forgetting that outflow to stock is a negative link.","Counting only the policy links and missing the final link back into the stock.","Saying a balancing loop always stabilises: with delays it can oscillate.","Not tracing an actual increase (the question says \"confirm polarity by tracing\")."]},{"id":"r0-herd-immunity","title":"R0, transmission rate, force of infection, Rn and herd immunity threshold","priority":7,"tier":"core","freq":"est. very high (Lecture 9 challenges 10.1; Sheet 1 Q3a)","marks":"5 marks","summary":"**beta = R0/D, force of infection lambda = beta x I/N, Rn = R0 x S/N, herd immunity threshold HIT = 1 - 1/R0: an epidemic grows only while Rn > 1, and vaccinating a fraction above HIT stops it.**","mnemonic":"BETA = R0 over D; LAMBDA = BETA times I/N; HIT = 1 minus 1 over R0","source":["CT561_Notes_09_Disease_Modelling_2","CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions and formulas","items":["**R0 (basic reproduction number)**: average number of secondary infections one infectious person produces in a **totally susceptible** population. R0 = c x i x d (contacts per day x probability of transmission per contact x duration of infectiousness).","**D** (or d): average duration of infectiousness = **recovery delay** (days); recovery rate = I/D.","**c**: contact rate (contacts per person per day). **i**: infectivity (probability that a contact with an infectious person transmits).",{"type":"formula","label":"Transmission rate beta (per day)","text":"beta = c x i = R0 / D"},{"type":"formula","label":"Force of infection lambda (per day, per susceptible)","text":"lambda = beta x I / N  (probability per day that a susceptible is infected)"},{"type":"formula","label":"Infection rate","text":"Infection Rate IR = S x lambda = beta x S x I / N = c x i x (S/N) x I"},"I/N is the **prevalence** (probability that a random contact is infectious); S/N is the chance that a contact is still susceptible.",{"type":"formula","label":"Net (effective) reproduction number","text":"Rn = R0 x (S/N)  (changes over time as S falls)"},"Rn: average number of secondary cases per infectious person when some individuals are already immune (by infection or vaccination). **Rn <= 1: no epidemic and the infectious stock falls to zero.**",{"type":"formula","label":"Herd immunity threshold","text":"HIT = 1 - 1 / R0"},"Derivation: Rn = 1 when S/N = 1/R0, so the susceptible fraction must be pushed below 1/R0, i.e. immune fraction above 1 - 1/R0. Vaccination coverage above HIT makes substantial outbreaks unlikely (assumes a perfect vaccine and random mixing).","Examples: R0 = 2: HIT 50%. R0 = 4: 75%. R0 = 12 (measles): 91.7%. R0 = 14: 92.9%. Higher R0 means a higher threshold."]},{"heading":"Worked example: Lecture challenge 10.1","items":["N = 100,000, 1% infectious (I = 1000), R0 = 14, D = 7 days.","beta = R0/D = 14/7 = **2 per day**. lambda = beta x I/N = 2 x 1000/100000 = **0.02 per day**. (Infection rate = S x lambda = 99,000 x 0.02 = 1,980 people per day.)"]},{"heading":"Worked example: Sheet 1 Q3(a) measles","items":["N = 10,000, 2% infectious (I = 200), R0 = 12, D = 7 days.","beta = 12/7 = 1.714 per day. lambda = 1.714 x 200/10000 = **0.0343 per day** (3.4% of susceptibles infected per day).","HIT = 1 - 1/12 = 0.9167 = **91.7%**, i.e. about 9,167 of 10,000 must be immune."]},{"heading":"Interpretation","items":["If Rn > 1 the infected stock rises; Rn = 1 at the peak; Rn < 1 after the peak.","In the SIR simulation (R0 = 2, N = 10,000) the peak occurs when S/N = 1/R0, i.e. S = 5000 (it is about 5135 at the peak at t = 18, matching).","R0 describes the pathogen in a given population and behaviour, not a constant of nature; measures (c, i, d) change the effective R.","With imperfect vaccine efficacy E the coverage needed is HIT/E (extra, not from the notes)."]},{"heading":"Herd immunity: lecture slide data and the HIT curve","items":["Lecture slide quoted from Fine, Eames and Heymann (\"Herd immunity: a rough guide\"): vaccination programmes aim for coverage **above** the HIT, because if the immune proportion is above it, substantial outbreaks are unlikely.",{"type":"table","head":["Infection","Serial interval","R0","Herd immunity (slide)"],"rows":[["Diphtheria","2-30 days","6-7","85%"],["Influenza","2-4 days","2-4","50-75%"],["Malaria","20 days","5-100","80-99%"],["Measles","7-16 days","12-18","83-94%"],["Pertussis","5-35 days","12-17","92-94%"]]},"The slide values are empirical ranges; the formula HIT = 1 - 1/R0 gives 91.7% (R0 12) to 94.4% (R0 18) for measles. Higher R0 means a higher threshold, but the curve flattens: going from R0 5 to R0 10 adds only 10 points, whereas R0 1 to 2 adds 50.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"line","data":{"title":"Herd immunity threshold against R0 (HIT = 1 - 1/R0)","xLabel":"R0","yLabel":"HIT (%)","yMin":0,"yMax":100,"series":[{"name":"HIT","points":[[1,0],[1.5,33.3],[2,50],[3,66.7],[4,75],[5,80],[6,83.3],[8,87.5],[10,90],[12,91.7],[15,93.3],[20,95]]}]},"caption":"Shape to sketch: starts at 0 when R0 = 1, rises steeply, then flattens towards 100% (it never reaches 100%). Influenza A sits low on the curve, rubella in the middle, measles near the top."}]}],"pitfalls":["Using beta = R0 x D (should be R0 / D).","Dividing by S instead of N in the force of infection.","Quoting HIT as 1/R0 (that is the susceptible threshold; HIT is its complement).","Forgetting units (per day) or giving the HIT as a fraction without saying so.","Confusing R0 (fully susceptible) with Rn (current)."]},{"id":"sir-model","title":"SIR model: structure, equations and epidemic curve","priority":8,"tier":"core","freq":"est. high (basis of every disease question)","marks":"8-15 marks","summary":"**SIR splits a closed population N into Susceptible, Infected and Recovered stocks: S falls by IR = beta S I/N, I rises by IR and falls by I/D, R rises by I/D; it has a Contagion (R) loop and Depletion and Recovery (B) loops.**","mnemonic":"S gives to I gives to R; one R loop (contagion), two B loops (depletion, recovery)","source":["CT561_Notes_09_Disease_Modelling_2","CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025","CT561_Problem_Sheet_02_2025-2026"],"sections":[{"heading":"Structure and equations","items":["Assumptions: closed population (no births, deaths, migration), homogeneous mixing, recovered are immune, constant parameters, deterministic.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"SIR compartment flow","height":200,"nodes":[{"id":"S","label":"S","sub":"Susceptible","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"I","label":"I","sub":"Infected","x":50,"y":50,"color":2},{"id":"R","label":"R","sub":"Recovered","x":90,"y":50,"color":1}],"edges":[{"from":"S","to":"I","label":"IR = beta S I/N"},{"from":"I","to":"R","label":"RR = I/D"}]}},{"type":"formula","label":"Differential form","text":"dS/dt = -beta S I/N;  dI/dt = beta S I/N - I/D;  dR/dt = I/D"},{"type":"formula","label":"Difference (Euler) form","text":"S(t+DT) = S(t) - DT x IR(t);  I(t+DT) = I(t) + DT x (IR(t) - RR(t));  R(t+DT) = R(t) + DT x RR(t)"},"Parameters: beta = R0/D, lambda = beta I/N, IR = S lambda, RR = I/D. Initial: I(0) = I0, S(0) = N - I0, R(0) = 0. S + I + R = N always.","**Attack rate** = proportion of the population that has been infected = (N - S)/N = (I + R)/N (with quarantine and vaccination: (N - S - V)/N).","**Feedback loops**: **Contagion (R)**: more I, more infection, more I. **Depletion (B)**: more infection, fewer S, lower infection. **Recovery (B)**: more I, more recovery, fewer I."]},{"heading":"Worked Euler steps (N = 1000, I0 = 10, R0 = 3, D = 4, DT = 1)","items":["beta = 3/4 = 0.75. Step 0: IR = 0.75 x 990 x 10/1000 = 7.425; RR = 10/4 = 2.5. So S = 982.58, I = 10 + 7.425 - 2.5 = 14.93, R = 2.5.","Step 1: IR = 0.75 x 982.58 x 14.93/1000 = 11.00; RR = 3.73. S = 971.58, I = 22.19, R = 6.23.","Step 2: IR = 16.17; RR = 5.55. S = 955.41, I = 32.82, R = 11.78 (check: sums to 1000)."]},{"heading":"Epidemic curve (N = 10,000, I0 = 1, R0 = 2, D = 2 days)","items":["Computed with DT = 0.01. Peak: about 1537 infected at t = 18.2 days. Final: S about 2029, attack rate about 79.7% (the final size equation z = 1 - e^(-R0 z) gives 0.797).","Phases: slow exponential start (contagion dominates), peak when Rn = 1 (S = N/R0 = 5000), then decline (depletion and recovery dominate). The epidemic ends with many still susceptible: it dies out because Rn < 1, not because S is zero.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"line","data":{"title":"SIR epidemic curve (R0 = 2, D = 2, N = 10000, I0 = 1)","xLabel":"Days","yLabel":"People","series":[{"name":"Susceptible","points":[[0,9999],[2,9996],[4,9986],[6,9961],[8,9894],[10,9720],[12,9289],[14,8364],[16,6842],[18,5135],[20,3816],[22,3009],[24,2561],[26,2319],[28,2187],[30,2116],[32,2077],[34,2055],[36,2043],[38,2037],[40,2033]]},{"name":"Infected","points":[[0,1],[2,3],[4,7],[6,20],[8,53],[10,139],[12,343],[14,743],[16,1262],[18,1535],[20,1370],[22,990],[24,633],[26,378],[28,218],[30,123],[32,69],[34,38],[36,21],[38,12],[40,6]]},{"name":"Recovered","points":[[0,0],[2,2],[4,6],[6,19],[8,53],[10,142],[12,368],[14,893],[16,1896],[18,3330],[20,4814],[22,6001],[24,6806],[26,7303],[28,7595],[30,7761],[32,7855],[34,7907],[36,7936],[38,7952],[40,7960]]}]}},"Effect of R0 (computed attack rates, peak infected): R0 = 1: about 0.9% (no epidemic); 1.5: 58.3% (peak 631); 2: 79.7% (1537); 3: 94.1% (3011); 4: 98.0% (4044).","Euler accuracy: the same model with DT = 1 gives a peak of about 1721 at t = 22 and attack rate 82.8%, noticeably different from DT = 0.01: choose DT small relative to D."]}],"pitfalls":["Writing IR = beta x S x I (missing the /N) which breaks the units.","Forgetting S + I + R = N when initialising (S0 = N - I0).","Stating that the epidemic ends when everyone has been infected: it ends when Rn drops below 1.","Mislabelling loops: contagion is the only reinforcing loop."]},{"id":"basic-flow-structures","title":"Fractional increase, fractional decrease and adjustment to a goal","priority":9,"tier":"core","freq":"est. high (Sheet 1 Q2a 6 marks; Sheet 2 Q2a 8 marks)","marks":"6-8 marks","summary":"**Fractional increase (inflow = Stock x fraction) gives exponential growth (R loop); fractional decrease (outflow = Stock x fraction, or Stock/lifetime) gives exponential decay (B loop); adjustment to a goal (flow = (Goal - Stock)/AT) gives S-shaped or goal-seeking behaviour (B loop).**","mnemonic":"Grow by fraction R, decay by fraction B, chase a goal B","source":["CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025","CT561_Problem_Sheet_02_2025-2026"],"sections":[{"heading":"Fractional increase rate","items":[{"type":"formula","label":"Fractional increase","text":"Inflow = Stock x g,  g = fractional growth rate (per time unit)"},"Reinforcing loop: bigger stock, bigger inflow, bigger stock. Behaviour: **exponential growth**, Stock(t) = Stock(0) e^(g t); doubling time about 0.69 / g.","Examples: population births (births = population x birth fraction), compound interest, early epidemic growth (contagion loop), viral content.","Numeric: Stock = 100, g = 0.10 per year, DT = 1: inflow 10, then stock 110 gives inflow 11, stock 121, inflow 12.1, stock 133.1."]},{"heading":"Fractional decrease rate","items":[{"type":"formula","label":"Fractional decrease","text":"Outflow = Stock x f = Stock / L,  f = fractional decrease rate, L = 1/f = average lifetime"},"Balancing loop: bigger stock, bigger outflow, smaller stock. Behaviour: **exponential decay** towards zero, Stock(t) = Stock(0) e^(-f t); half-life about 0.69 L.","Examples: staff quits (quits = staff x quit fraction), recovery (recovery = Infected / D), radioactive decay, depreciation, customer churn.","Numeric: Stock = 100, f = 0.2 per month, DT = 1: outflow 20, stock 80, outflow 16, stock 64, outflow 12.8, stock 51.2."]},{"heading":"Adjustment to a goal (goal seeking)","items":[{"type":"formula","label":"Adjustment to a goal","text":"Net flow = (Goal - Stock) / AT,  AT = adjustment time (time constant)"},"Discrepancy = Goal - Stock; the flow closes a fraction 1/AT of the gap each time unit. Balancing loop. Behaviour: **goal-seeking, exponential approach** that is fast at first and slows as the gap closes; about 63% of the gap closed after one AT and 95% after 3 AT.","Examples: room temperature to thermostat setting, workforce moving to a target, pressure to clean up the environment, a vaccination fraction moving to its maximum.","Numeric: Goal = 100, Stock = 20, AT = 4, DT = 1: flow 20 then stock 40, flow 15 then stock 55, flow 11.25 then stock 66.25, flow 8.4 then stock 74.7.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"line","data":{"title":"The three structures, each starting at 20 (goal 100, rate 0.1, AT 4)","xLabel":"Time","yLabel":"Stock","series":[{"name":"Fractional increase (g = 0.1)","points":[[0,20],[2,24.4],[4,29.8],[6,36.4],[8,44.5],[10,54.4],[12,66.4],[14,81.1],[16,99.1]]},{"name":"Fractional decrease (f = 0.1)","points":[[0,20],[2,16.4],[4,13.4],[6,11],[8,9],[10,7.4],[12,6],[14,4.9],[16,4]]},{"name":"Adjustment to goal (AT = 4)","points":[[0,20],[2,51.5],[4,70.6],[6,82.1],[8,89.2],[10,93.4],[12,96],[14,97.6],[16,98.5]]}]},"caption":"Continuous-time values computed from the exact solutions."}]},{"heading":"Expected enrolments structure (Sheet 2 Q4a, 5 marks)","items":["Given: stock **Students** with inflow **Enrolments** and outflow **Graduations** (both clouds). Task: add a stock and flow structure, with equations, that models **expected enrolments**.","Idea: managers do not see the true future inflow; they form an expectation by **smoothing past enrolments** (first-order information delay, an adjustment-to-a-goal where the goal is the actual Enrolments). Expected Enrolments can then drive planning (for example Desired Staff = Expected Enrolments x programme length / student-staff ratio).",{"type":"table","head":["Variable","Equation","Initial"],"rows":[["Students (stock)","Students + DT x (Enrolments - Graduations)","400 (example)"],["Graduations","Students / Programme Duration (a material delay, 4 years)","100 per year"],["Enrolments","Applications x Acceptance Fraction, or an exogenous input (step/ramp)","100 per year"],["Expected Enrolments (new stock)","Expected + DT x Change in Expected Enrolments","100 = Enrolments (start in equilibrium)"],["Change in Expected Enrolments (new flow)","(Enrolments - Expected Enrolments) / Time to Perceive Enrolments","0"],["Time to Perceive Enrolments","constant, e.g. 3 years","3"]]},"Link polarities: Enrolments to Change in Expected +; Expected Enrolments to Change in Expected - (balancing loop, goal seeking: the expectation chases reality); Time to Perceive to Change -. Expected Enrolments to whatever it drives (+).","Numeric check (Enrolments steps from 100 to 120 at year 2, time to perceive 3, DT = 1): Expected = 100, 100, 100, 106.67, 111.11, 114.07 at years 0-5, closing 1/3 of the gap each year and lagging reality (63% after one time constant).","Alternative accepted structure: Expected Enrolments as a trend or as an adjustment to a demand goal (Applications), same form: change = (goal - expected)/adjustment time."]},{"heading":"Varying decrease fraction (Sheet 2 Q2a)","items":["Problem: stock S with outflow = S x G where the fraction G itself changes over time, moving to a maximum G* with adjustment constant AT.",{"type":"formula","label":"Equations","text":"Outflow = S x G;  G(t + DT) = G(t) + DT x (G* - G)/AT;  G(0) = initial fraction (for example 0)"},"Treat G as a second stock: its only flow is the adjustment inflow (G* - G)/AT, a balancing goal-seeking loop. S has the usual balancing loop (S up, outflow up, S down). G is not affected by S, so G is exogenous to the S loop.","Real-world use: **vaccination fraction** ramping up as capacity is built, the staff quit fraction rising as morale falls, a policy whose effect phases in gradually, customers adopting a technology, drug resistance building up, or a person gradually changing behaviour. It models a policy or behaviour that does not switch on instantly but is implemented gradually.","Effect: slow start (G small so the outflow is small), then the decay accelerates as G rises, then levels at the rate set by G*; S follows an S-like reduction curve rather than a pure exponential."]}],"pitfalls":["Writing Goal - Stock the wrong way round (flow would push the stock away from its goal).","Calling goal seeking \"linear\": it is exponential approach, never actually reaching the goal in finite time.","Forgetting to say which loop type each structure creates (increase = R, decrease = B, goal = B).","For the varying fraction question, forgetting that G needs its own initial value and its own equation."]},{"id":"delays-smoothing","title":"Delays: first-order smoothing and material delays","priority":10,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. medium (anchor delay structure; pharma progression delays)","marks":"3-5 marks","summary":"**A first-order delay is a stock draining at Stock/Delay Time: an information delay smooths a signal (Smoothed changes by (Input - Smoothed)/ST), a material delay holds items for an average time (Stock = Inflow x Delay at steady state).**","mnemonic":"Outflow = Stock / Delay; average residence time = Delay","source":["CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025","CT561_Problem_Sheet_02_2025-2026"],"sections":[{"heading":"Material delay (first order)","items":["A stock between two flows: Outflow = Stock / Delay Time. Items stay on average Delay Time (average residence time).",{"type":"formula","label":"First-order material delay","text":"Outflow = Stock / D;  steady state: Stock = Inflow x D"},"Example: Trainees progress to Experienced at Trainee / 12 per month (progression delay 12 months). With 100 trainees, progression = 8.33 per month.","It is a fractional decrease flow with fraction 1/D; step response of the output is an exponential approach with time constant D."]},{"heading":"Information delay (smoothing)","items":[{"type":"formula","label":"Exponential smoothing","text":"Smoothed(t + DT) = Smoothed(t) + DT x (Input - Smoothed(t))/ST,  ST = smoothing (perception) time"},"Models how perceptions, expectations or anchors lag reality. Smoothed value moves 63% towards a step change in one ST.","Material is conserved in a material delay (output eventually equals input); an information delay only passes on a number, nothing is conserved.","Higher-order delays are chains of first-order stocks; they make the output response S-shaped and sharper."]},{"heading":"Why delays matter","items":["In a balancing loop, delays can cause **overshoot and oscillation** (acting on stale information).","In the stock management structure the anchor smoothing and hiring/training delays slow the response.","Euler needs DT well below the smallest delay (a quarter or less)."]}],"pitfalls":["Using Stock x Delay for the outflow (should be divided).","Treating the smoothing time as the time to reach the goal: about 63% after one ST and 95% after three."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Material delay","items":["Holds items for a while","Stock = Inflow x Delay at steady state"]},{"title":"Information delay","items":["Smooths a signal","Change = (Input - Smoothed) / ST"]}]}}},{"id":"cohort-models","title":"Cohort (age-structured) disease models","priority":11,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. medium-high (Sheet 1 Q3c, 5 marks)","marks":"5 marks","summary":"**To represent young and elderly cohorts, replicate the S, I, R stocks (and flows) for each cohort using subscripts, give each cohort its own parameters, and make each cohort's force of infection a sum over the infectious people in all cohorts via a contact matrix.**","mnemonic":"Copy the structure per cohort, then couple them through the contact matrix","source":["CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025"],"sections":[{"heading":"How to modify the model","items":["1. **Subscript** every stock and flow by cohort j in {Young, Elderly}: S_j, I_j, R_j (and Q_j, V_j if used); N_j = cohort size.","2. **Cohort-specific parameters**: initial sizes, recovery delay D_j, quarantine fraction, vaccination fraction (vaccination often targeted at the elderly), possibly infectivity, and mortality (extra Died stock for the elderly).","3. **Coupling by a contact matrix**: c_jk = contacts per day of an individual in cohort j with individuals of cohort k.",{"type":"formula","label":"Cohort force of infection","text":"lambda_j = i x SUM over k of [ c_jk x I_k / N_k ];  IR_j = S_j x lambda_j;  RR_j = I_j / D_j"},"4. Keep the total balanced: S_j + I_j + R_j = N_j; add ageing flows between cohorts only for long-term models.","5. Run the model by cohort and compare outcomes (attack rate by age group, deaths, effect of targeted vaccination)."]},{"heading":"Why and trade-offs","items":["Why: the real population is heterogeneous: young people have more contacts (school), the elderly have a higher risk of severe disease. A single homogeneous mixing assumption cannot represent these differences or evaluate targeted policies.","Costs: more stocks and parameters, need for age-specific contact data, harder to calibrate and explain; same equations, so Vensim subscripts / Python arrays make it manageable.","The feedback structure inside each cohort is unchanged (contagion, depletion, recovery); new reinforcing links appear between cohorts (infection in one raises infection in the other)."]},{"heading":"Diagram","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"Two cohorts coupled through contacts","height":300,"nodes":[{"id":"Sy","label":"S young","x":5,"y":15},{"id":"Iy","label":"I young","x":40,"y":15,"color":2},{"id":"Ry","label":"R young","x":80,"y":15,"color":1},{"id":"Se","label":"S elderly","x":5,"y":85},{"id":"Ie","label":"I elderly","x":40,"y":85,"color":2},{"id":"Re","label":"R elderly","x":80,"y":85,"color":1}],"edges":[{"from":"Sy","to":"Iy"},{"from":"Iy","to":"Ry"},{"from":"Se","to":"Ie"},{"from":"Ie","to":"Re"},{"from":"Iy","to":"Se","label":"contacts","curve":20},{"from":"Ie","to":"Sy","label":"contacts","curve":20}]}}]}],"pitfalls":["Simply duplicating the model with no coupling (cohorts must infect each other through contacts).","Dividing by total N instead of cohort sizes without saying so; state the convention.","Forgetting that parameters (vaccination, D, mortality) can differ by cohort, which is the point of the extension."]},{"id":"stocks-flows-basics","title":"Stock and flow diagrams: notation and conventions","priority":12,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. high (asked in every model-building question)","marks":"3-5 marks per diagram","summary":"**Stocks (boxes) accumulate; flows (valves on pipes) change stocks; clouds mark the system boundary; auxiliaries (variables) and constants feed flows via information links, never directly into stocks.**","mnemonic":"Boxes hold, valves move, clouds bound, arrows inform","source":["CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025","CT561_Problem_Sheet_02_2025-2026","CT561_Notes_09_Disease_Modelling_2"],"sections":[{"heading":"Building blocks","items":["**Stock** (rectangle): a quantity that accumulates and can be measured at one instant (population, backlog, staff, bank balance). Only changed by flows; has an initial value.","**Flow** (pipe with valve): rate of change per unit time (people/day). Inflow adds, outflow subtracts. A flow has units of stock/time.","**Cloud**: a source or sink outside the model boundary (infinite supply or disposal).","**Auxiliary / variable**: calculated from other variables at each time step (for example Work Pressure, force of infection).","**Constant / parameter**: fixed input (R0, D, N, adjustment time).","**Information link (thin arrow)**: shows that one variable is used in the equation of another; it does not move material. Material only moves along flow pipes.","Conservation: in the SIR model S + I + R = N always (closed population, flows move people between stocks)."]},{"heading":"Rules for drawing a correct model","items":["Every stock needs at least one flow; every flow needs a stock at one end or a cloud.","Units must match: flow = quantity / time.","Links go from the variable used to the variable that uses it, with a polarity (+/-) when you are asked for feedback.","Use a **flag** (0 or 1) to switch a structure on or off: effective value = parameter x flag.","Initial values: stocks need them (S = N - I0, I = I0, R = 0); an equilibrium start means inflows = outflows at t = 0."]},{"heading":"Typical equations","items":[{"type":"formula","label":"Stock equation (Euler form)","text":"Stock(t + DT) = Stock(t) + DT x (Inflow - Outflow)"},{"type":"formula","label":"Continuous form","text":"d(Stock)/dt = Inflow - Outflow, Stock(0) = initial value"},{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"Generic stock and flow","height":200,"nodes":[{"id":"c1","label":"src","x":5,"y":50},{"id":"st","label":"Stock","sub":"initial value","x":50,"y":50,"color":1},{"id":"c2","label":"snk","x":95,"y":50},{"id":"aux","label":"Net Flow","x":50,"y":5,"color":2}],"edges":[{"from":"c1","to":"st","label":"Inflow"},{"from":"st","to":"c2","label":"Outflow"},{"from":"st","to":"aux","curve":25}]},"caption":"Material flows along the pipes; the net flow auxiliary is calculated from the flows (draw links from both flows to it)."}]}],"pitfalls":["Drawing an information arrow straight into a stock: stocks change only through flows.","Forgetting clouds or giving a flow no units.","Omitting initial values and parameters in \"design a model with equations\" questions."]},{"id":"threshold-policies","title":"Threshold dynamics and the \"battle of the flows\" policies","priority":13,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. medium (Lecture 9 slides 4-8)","marks":"4-6 marks","summary":"**The infectious stock rises only if the infection inflow exceeds the recovery outflow, which reduces to R0 S/N > 1; every public health policy either reduces the inflow (c, i, S) or increases the outflow (shorter d).**","mnemonic":"Win the Battle of the Flows: cut the inflow (c, i, S) or speed the outflow (d)","source":["CT561_Notes_09_Disease_Modelling_2"],"sections":[{"heading":"Threshold derivation","items":["Infectious stock: dI/dt = Inflow - Outflow = c i (S/N) I - I/d.","Equilibrium (I does not rise or fall): c i S/N I = I/d, so c i S/N = 1/d, i.e. c i d S/N = 1, i.e. R0 S/N = 1.","For I to increase: inflow > outflow, c i (S/N) I > I/d. In a totally susceptible population (S = N): c i > 1/d, so c i d > 1, so **R0 > 1**. In general Rn = R0 S/N > 1.","Threshold on S: the epidemic grows if S/N > 1/R0 and declines if S/N < 1/R0."]},{"heading":"Mapping the flow equation onto policy","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Policy type","Also known as","Parameter changed","Battle of the flows","Outcome"],"rows":[["Non-pharmaceutical","Restrictions, distancing, masks","c (reduce contacts), i (reduce transmission)","Reduce the inflow","Stock of infectious falls"],["Non-pharmaceutical","Test-Trace-Isolate (quarantine)","d (reduce time infectious and circulating)","Increase the outflow","Stock of infectious falls"],["Pharmaceutical","Antivirals (e.g. influenza)","i (reduce viral load)","Reduce the inflow","Stock of infectious falls"],["Pharmaceutical","Vaccination","S (reduce susceptibles)","Reduce the inflow","Stock of infectious falls"]]},"In the equation c i S/N = 1/d: policies on the left (c, i, S) push the inflow down; policies on the right (shorter d) push the outflow up.","Quarantine in the exam model: infected people in quarantine do not transmit, so they are removed from the infection force (reduces effective duration of circulation)."]}],"pitfalls":["Saying vaccination increases the outflow: it reduces S, so it reduces the inflow.","Forgetting that quarantine acts on d (time circulating), not on c or i."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Epidemic grows if","items":["R0 x S / N > 1"]},{"title":"Cut the inflow","items":["Fewer contacts c","Lower infectivity i","Fewer susceptibles S"]},{"title":"Raise the outflow","items":["Shorter infectious time d"]}]}}},{"id":"sensitivity-analysis","title":"Sensitivity analysis / parameter sweeps","priority":14,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. medium (Lecture challenge 10.2; Sheet 2 Q2c)","marks":"5 marks","summary":"**Sensitivity analysis runs the model repeatedly while one or two parameters vary over a range (for example QF from 0 to 1) and records an outcome (attack rate, peak) to see which parameters matter.**","mnemonic":"Vary, rerun, record, compare","source":["CT561_Notes_09_Disease_Modelling_2","CT561_Problem_Sheet_02_2025-2026"],"sections":[{"heading":"Method","items":["Choose the parameter(s) and a range or grid (QF in [0,1]; QF x VF grid).","Loop in code (Python in the lecture notebook; Vensim has sensitivity tools): set parameters, run to the end, store the output (final attack rate, peak infected, time of peak).","Plot output against parameter (line for one parameter; bubble or heat map for two).","Interpret: which parameter changes the outcome most, thresholds (R0 (1 - QF) < 1 means almost no outbreak), interactions between parameters, and robustness of conclusions to uncertainty."]},{"heading":"Example: attack rate vs QF (R0 = 2, D = 2, N = 10,000, I0 = 1, no vaccination)","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"line","data":{"title":"Final attack rate vs quarantine fraction","xLabel":"QF","yLabel":"Attack rate","series":[{"name":"Attack rate","points":[[0,0.797],[0.1,0.733],[0.2,0.642],[0.3,0.511],[0.4,0.308],[0.5,0.009],[0.6,0.001]]}]},"caption":"Computed by simulation (Euler, DT = 0.01). The curve collapses near QF = 0.5 because R0 (1 - QF) = 1: a threshold."}]}],"pitfalls":["Varying several parameters at once and then attributing the effect to one.","Reporting a single run when the question asks about the relationship across the range."]},{"id":"seir-exploratory","title":"SEIR and the exploratory model with clinical and sub-clinical streams","priority":15,"tier":"likely","freq":"est. medium (Lecture challenge 10.3)","marks":"8-12 marks","summary":"**SEIR adds an Exposed (latent, not yet infectious) stock between S and I; the exploratory model splits infections into clinical (pre-clinical then clinical, 60%) and sub-clinical (40%, half as infectious) streams, and three infectious compartments feed the force of infection.**","mnemonic":"S, then E (latent), then split: clinical (pre, clinical) or sub-clinical, then R","source":["CT561_Notes_09_Disease_Modelling_2"],"sections":[{"heading":"SEIR basics","items":["**E (Exposed)**: infected but not yet infectious (latent period). Flow E to I = E / Latent Time. Infection rate = S x lambda feeds E, not I.",{"type":"formula","label":"SEIR","text":"dS/dt = -beta S I/N;  dE/dt = beta S I/N - E/L;  dI/dt = E/L - I/D;  dR/dt = I/D  (L = latent time)"},"The latent delay slows the early growth and delays the peak compared with SIR. Same R0 = beta D and the same final size."]},{"heading":"Challenge 10.3 parameters","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Parameter","Meaning","Value"],"rows":[["Latent Time","time in Exposed stock","3 days"],["Pre-Infectious Clinical Time","time pre-clinical (already spreading)","2.1 days"],["Infectious Time (Clinical)","time infectious with symptoms","2.9 days"],["Infectious Time (Sub-clinical)","time infectious without symptoms","5 days"],["Clinical Fraction","proportion of infected who show symptoms","0.60"],["Contacts","contacts per person per day","10"],["Infectivity","probability of transmission per contact","0.06"],["Transmission multiplier","sub-clinical relative infectiousness","0.50"]]},"Population about 5 million, 10 initially infected (put in Exposed in the simulation below)."]},{"heading":"Structure and equations","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"SEIR with clinical and sub-clinical streams","height":360,"nodes":[{"id":"S","label":"S","x":3,"y":50},{"id":"E","label":"E","sub":"Exposed","x":25,"y":50,"color":3},{"id":"P","label":"Pre-clinical","x":52,"y":15,"color":2},{"id":"C","label":"Clinical","x":75,"y":15,"color":2},{"id":"U","label":"Sub-clinical","x":62,"y":85,"color":2},{"id":"R","label":"R","x":97,"y":50,"color":1}],"edges":[{"from":"S","to":"E","label":"IR"},{"from":"E","to":"P","label":"CF x E/L"},{"from":"P","to":"C","label":"P/2.1"},{"from":"C","to":"R","label":"C/2.9"},{"from":"E","to":"U","label":"(1-CF) E/L"},{"from":"U","to":"R","label":"U/5"}]}},{"type":"formula","label":"Force of infection (three infectious compartments)","text":"lambda = c x i x (Pre-clinical + Clinical + Multiplier x Sub-clinical) / N"},"Infection Rate = S x lambda. Flows: E to Pre-clinical = Clinical Fraction x E/Latent; E to Sub-clinical = (1 - CF) x E/Latent; Pre-clinical to Clinical = P/2.1; Clinical to R = C/2.9; Sub-clinical to R = U/5.",{"type":"formula","label":"R0 for this model","text":"R0 = c i [ CF (2.1 + 2.9) + (1 - CF) x 0.5 x 5 ] = 0.6 x [0.6 x 5 + 0.4 x 2.5] = 0.6 x 4 = 2.4"},"Simulation (DT 0.01 days): peak total infectious about 757,000 at day 89; E peaks about 431,000; attack rate about 87.9%.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"line","data":{"title":"Exploratory SEIR model: Exposed and Infectious (thousands)","xLabel":"Days","yLabel":"Thousands of people","series":[{"name":"Exposed","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[20,0.1],[30,0.4],[40,2],[50,9.1],[60,40.6],[70,161.1],[80,419.1],[90,430.8],[100,181.6],[110,53.2],[120,14.5],[130,3.9],[140,1.1],[150,0.3]]},{"name":"Infectious (all three)","points":[[0,0],[10,0],[20,0.1],[30,0.4],[40,2],[50,9.1],[60,41.1],[70,170.3],[80,518.4],[90,753.1],[100,461.7],[110,169.7],[120,51],[130,14.3],[140,4],[150,1.1]]}]}}]}],"pitfalls":["Feeding the infection flow into I instead of E.","Leaving pre-clinical people out of the force of infection (they spread before symptoms).","Forgetting the transmission multiplier for sub-clinical cases."]},{"id":"disease-model-variants","title":"Compartment model variants, assumptions and limitations","priority":16,"tier":"possible","freq":"est. medium (supplementary to SIR; discuss-style questions)","marks":"5-8 marks","summary":"**SIR (permanent immunity), SIS (no immunity, endemic equilibrium) and SEIR (latent period) are compartment models; all assume homogeneous mixing and constant parameters, so real-world use needs extensions (cohorts, networks, stochasticity, behaviour).**","mnemonic":"SIR immune, SIS return, SEIR wait","source":["CT561_Notes_09_Disease_Modelling_2","CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025"],"sections":[{"heading":"Variants","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Model","Flow","Use / feature"],"rows":[["SIR","S to I to R","permanent immunity (measles, a one-season flu); epidemic then extinction"],["SIS","S to I to S","no lasting immunity (common cold, gonorrhoea); endemic equilibrium I* = N (1 - 1/R0) if R0 > 1"],["SEIR","S to E to I to R","latent period before infectiousness (measles, COVID)"],["SIQR / SIRV","adds Q or V","countermeasures: quarantine, vaccination"]]},"SIS example: N = 1000, R0 = 3 gives I* = 1000 x (1 - 1/3) = 667 and S* = N/R0 = 333; Rn = 1 at equilibrium (infection inflow balances recovery).","Difference-equation form is what a simulation uses (Euler); the differential form is the continuous model."]},{"heading":"Assumptions and limitations","items":["Homogeneous mixing (everyone has the same contact rate); real contacts are clustered by age, place and network.","Closed population; no births, deaths or migration (add them for long-term models).","Constant parameters; real behaviour changes (people distance when infections rise: a balancing loop not in basic SIR).","Deterministic: with few initial cases chance matters (random extinction).","Lifelong immunity in SIR; waning immunity needs SIRS.","Aggregation: individuals are not tracked (no memory or history); compartments average everything.","Detailed data needed for calibration (R0 uncertain); results sensitive to parameter values."]}],"pitfalls":["Stating SIR is wrong rather than saying which assumptions fail and what extension fixes them."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"SIR","items":["Immunity is permanent"]},{"title":"SIS","items":["No immunity","Endemic equilibrium"]},{"title":"SEIR","items":["Adds a latent period"]},{"title":"All assume","items":["Homogeneous mixing","Constant parameters"]}]}}},{"id":"implementation-tools","title":"Implementations: Vensim and Python","priority":17,"tier":"low","freq":"est. low (Lecture overview only)","marks":"0-3 marks","summary":"**Models are built either graphically in Vensim (stock and flow diagram with equations, built-in Euler integration, sensitivity tools) or as code in Python (explicit time loop updating stocks; easy parameter sweeps).**","mnemonic":"Vensim draws, Python loops","source":["CT561_Notes_09_Disease_Modelling_2"],"sections":[{"heading":"Comparison","items":["**Vensim**: diagram-based system dynamics software; stocks, flows, variables with equations; subscripts for cohorts; built-in integration (Euler) with time step setting; sensitivity analysis; good for communication of feedback structure.","**Python**: write the model as code: arrays for stocks, loop over time steps t = 0, DT, 2 DT ..., compute flows then update stocks (Euler); loops make parameter sweeps (lecture notebook varies QF) and plots easy.","Python skeleton: compute beta = R0/D; per step: lambda = beta*I/N; IR = S*lambda; S -= DT*IR; I += DT*(IR - I/D); R += DT*I/D.","Both implement the same equations; the choice is about speed of building, transparency and scripting."]}],"pitfalls":["Updating stocks one by one inside the loop using already-updated values: compute all flows first, then update all stocks."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Vensim","items":["Stock and flow diagram","Built-in Euler","Sensitivity tools"]},{"title":"Python","items":["Explicit time loop","Easy parameter sweeps"]}]}}},{"id":"bg-modelling-process","title":"Background: systems, models and simulation","priority":18,"tier":"possible","freq":"background (not in supplied notes)","marks":"3-5 marks","summary":"**A model is a simplified representation of a system built for a purpose; simulation runs it over time; system dynamics models feedback with stocks and flows, integrated numerically in small time steps.**","mnemonic":"Purpose, boundary, structure, equations, test, use","source":["CT561_Problem_Sheet_01_2024-2025"],"sections":[{"heading":"Basics (standard background)","items":["**System**: set of interacting parts with a purpose. **Model**: simplified abstraction answering a question. **Simulation**: executing a model over time to observe behaviour.","Modelling process: define purpose and boundary; identify variables; draw causal loops; stock and flow diagram; formulate equations and parameters; simulate; test (verification: does the code do what we meant; validation: does the model represent the real system well enough for its purpose); sensitivity analysis; use for policy experiments.","Continuous vs discrete: system dynamics treats quantities as continuous and uses numerical integration (Euler, DT); discrete-event and agent-based models change state at events or per agent.","Endogenous (generated by feedback inside the boundary) vs exogenous (inputs).","Behaviour modes: exponential growth, goal seeking, S-shaped growth, oscillation, overshoot and collapse.","Good models are simple enough to understand and detailed enough for the question (\"all models are wrong, some are useful\")."]}],"pitfalls":["Confusing verification (built right) with validation (right model)."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Real system","color":0},{"label":"Model","sub":"simplified, built for a purpose","color":1},{"label":"Simulate","sub":"run it over time","color":2},{"label":"Integrate","sub":"stocks and flows in small time steps","color":3}]}}},{"id":"bg-stochastic-abm","title":"Background: deterministic vs stochastic and agent-based models","priority":19,"tier":"possible","freq":"background (not in supplied notes)","marks":"3-5 marks","summary":"**Compartment (system dynamics) models are aggregate and deterministic (same inputs, same output); stochastic models include randomness (different runs), and agent-based models simulate individuals with their own rules and interactions.**","mnemonic":"Aggregate and deterministic vs random vs individual","source":["CT561_Notes_09_Disease_Modelling_2"],"sections":[{"heading":"Comparison (standard background)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Aspect","Deterministic compartment (SD)","Stochastic","Agent-based"],"rows":[["Unit","groups (stocks)","groups or individuals with random events","individual agents"],["Randomness","none: identical runs","yes: need many runs, distributions","usually yes"],["Heterogeneity","by adding cohorts","same","natural (each agent differs, networks)"],["Cost","cheap, fast","moderate","expensive, many parameters"],["Best for","large populations, policy feedback","small populations, extinction risk","spatial/network effects, behaviour"]]},"Stochastic effect: with I0 = 1 an outbreak can die out by chance even when R0 > 1 (the deterministic model always takes off).","Agent-based infection: each agent has a state and contacts; each contact transmits with probability i; emergent epidemic from many individual interactions."]}],"pitfalls":["Saying deterministic models are \"wrong\": they give the average behaviour and are right for large populations."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Deterministic","items":["Aggregate","Same inputs, same output"]},{"title":"Stochastic","items":["Randomness","Runs differ"]},{"title":"Agent-based","items":["Individuals with own rules"]}]}}}],"flashcards":[{"id":"eu-1","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Euler update equation?","back":"Stock(t+DT) = Stock(t) + DT x (Inflow(t) - Outflow(t)).","hook":"Left rectangle"},{"id":"eu-2","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Two assumptions of Euler's method?","back":"Net flow constant over each step (value at start of step); DT small enough."},{"id":"eu-3","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"When does Euler underestimate a stock?","back":"When the net flow is increasing over the step.","hook":"Rising: rectangle below"},{"id":"eu-4","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"When does Euler overestimate a stock?","back":"When the net flow is decreasing over the step."},{"id":"eu-5","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"What is the net flow?","back":"Sum of inflows minus sum of outflows; the slope of the stock."},{"id":"eu-6","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"What does graphical integration compute?","back":"The stock as the cumulative signed area under the net flow."},{"id":"eu-7","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Where does a stock peak or trough?","back":"Where the net flow crosses zero."},{"id":"eu-8","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Net flow positive and rising: stock behaviour?","back":"Rises at an increasing rate."},{"id":"eu-9","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Net flow positive and falling: stock behaviour?","back":"Rises at a decreasing rate."},{"id":"eu-10","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Net flow negative and rising towards zero?","back":"Stock falls at a decreasing rate."},{"id":"eu-11","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Sheet 1 Q4: net flow formula?","back":"Net = 50 - Outflow(t), from -50 to +50 and back; zero at t = 2.5, 7.5, ..."},{"id":"eu-12","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Sheet 1 Q4: stock extremes?","back":"Minimum 37.5 at t = 2.5 and 12.5; maximum 162.5 at t = 7.5 and 17.5; 100 at 0, 5, 10, 15, 20."},{"id":"eu-13","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Sheet 1 Q4c: Euler stock with DT = 10?","back":"100, -400, -900 at t = 0, 10, 20 (net = -50 each step)."},{"id":"eu-14","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Sheet 2 Q1: stock at turning points?","back":"Peak 162.5 (t = 2.5), trough -212.5 (t = 12.5), peak 162.5 (t = 22.5), 100 at t = 0, 5, 20, 25."},{"id":"eu-15","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Sheet 2 Q1c: Euler with DT = 5?","back":"350, 100, -150, 100, 350 at t = 5, 10, 15, 20, 25."},{"id":"eu-16","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Rule of thumb for DT?","back":"At most a quarter to a half of the smallest time constant in the model."},{"id":"sf-1","topicId":"stocks-flows-basics","front":"What is a stock?","back":"An accumulation that changes only through its flows (box)."},{"id":"sf-2","topicId":"stocks-flows-basics","front":"What is a flow?","back":"A rate of change per unit time (valve), moving material into or out of a stock."},{"id":"sf-3","topicId":"stocks-flows-basics","front":"What is a cloud in a stock-flow diagram?","back":"Source or sink outside the model boundary."},{"id":"sf-4","topicId":"stocks-flows-basics","front":"Can an information link point into a stock?","back":"No: stocks change only through flows."},{"id":"sf-5","topicId":"stocks-flows-basics","front":"What is a control flag?","back":"A 0/1 variable that switches a structure on/off (effective value = parameter x flag)."},{"id":"sf-6","topicId":"stocks-flows-basics","front":"Units of a flow?","back":"Stock units per time."},{"id":"bf-1","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"Fractional increase rate equation?","back":"Inflow = Stock x growth fraction; reinforcing; exponential growth."},{"id":"bf-2","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"Fractional decrease rate equation?","back":"Outflow = Stock x fraction = Stock / lifetime; balancing; exponential decay."},{"id":"bf-3","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"Adjustment to a goal equation?","back":"Net flow = (Goal - Stock)/AT; balancing; goal-seeking."},{"id":"bf-4","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"What is AT?","back":"Adjustment time: about 63% of the gap is closed after one AT, 95% after three."},{"id":"bf-5","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"Example of fractional decrease?","back":"Staff quits = Staff x quit fraction; recovery = I/D."},{"id":"bf-6","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"Example of fractional increase?","back":"Births = population x birth fraction; early epidemic growth."},{"id":"bf-7","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"Varying decrease fraction: structure?","back":"Outflow = S x G; G is a stock with flow (G* - G)/AT."},{"id":"bf-8","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"Use of a varying decrease fraction in practice?","back":"A policy or behaviour phased in gradually, e.g. a vaccination fraction ramping to its maximum."},{"id":"bf-9","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"Which loop type does each of the three structures give?","back":"Increase: R; decrease: B; goal seeking: B.","hook":"Grow R, decay B, chase B"},{"id":"fp-1","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Define feedback.","back":"A closed chain of causal links so a variable ultimately influences itself."},{"id":"fp-2","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Positive link polarity?","back":"Cause and effect move in the same direction."},{"id":"fp-3","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Negative link polarity?","back":"Cause and effect move in opposite directions."},{"id":"fp-4","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"How to find loop polarity?","back":"Count negative links: even = reinforcing, odd = balancing.","hook":"Even R, odd B"},{"id":"fp-5","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Behaviour of a reinforcing loop?","back":"Exponential growth or collapse (amplifies change)."},{"id":"fp-6","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Behaviour of a balancing loop?","back":"Goal seeking; with delay it can oscillate."},{"id":"fp-7","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Advantages of adding feedback to a stock-flow model?","back":"Endogenous behaviour, policy response, explains growth/oscillation, tests policies, reveals side effects."},{"id":"fp-8","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Sign of outflow to stock link?","back":"Negative (inflow to stock is positive)."},{"id":"fp-9","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Environment loop (Pressure, Cleanup Effort, Quality): type?","back":"Balancing: one negative link (Quality to Pressure); goal seeking."},{"id":"wl-1","topicId":"workload-backlog-models","front":"Policy loops in a backlog model: type?","back":"Balancing: raise completion to cut the backlog."},{"id":"wl-2","topicId":"workload-backlog-models","front":"Side-effect loops in a backlog model: type?","back":"Reinforcing: rework and fatigue push the backlog up again."},{"id":"wl-3","topicId":"workload-backlog-models","front":"Assignment model: three balancing loops?","back":"Work harder (study hours); cut time per assignment; deadline extension (time to submit)."},{"id":"wl-4","topicId":"workload-backlog-models","front":"Assignment model: two reinforcing loops?","back":"Rework (via time per assignment) and fatigue (via study hours)."},{"id":"wl-5","topicId":"workload-backlog-models","front":"Claims model: three side-effect loops?","back":"Rework from lower time per claim; temp staff raising time per claim; fatigue from longer workweek."},{"id":"wl-6","topicId":"workload-backlog-models","front":"Best response to rising workload (claims)?","back":"Temporary staff mainly, short workweek increase to cover delay, avoid cutting time per claim."},{"id":"wl-7","topicId":"workload-backlog-models","front":"Time to Submit loop: which variables?","back":"Work Pressure and Time to Submit only (+ then -): balancing."},{"id":"sm-1","topicId":"stock-management","front":"Stock management equation?","back":"Inflow = MAX(0, Anchor + Adjustment)."},{"id":"sm-2","topicId":"stock-management","front":"What is the anchor?","back":"Expected outflow: the flow that keeps the stock level when on target."},{"id":"sm-3","topicId":"stock-management","front":"What is the adjustment?","back":"(Desired Stock - Stock)/AT: closes the gap."},{"id":"sm-4","topicId":"stock-management","front":"Key difference from adjustment to a goal?","back":"Adds the anchor so the stock reaches the goal despite an outflow."},{"id":"sm-5","topicId":"stock-management","front":"Delay structure for the anchor?","back":"First-order smoothing: Expected Outflow changes by (Outflow - Expected)/smoothing time."},{"id":"sm-6","topicId":"stock-management","front":"Why MAX(0, ...)?","back":"Inflow cannot be negative (no un-hiring)."},{"id":"sm-7","topicId":"stock-management","front":"Which parameter sets the speed of the reset?","back":"The adjustment time AT."},{"id":"sm-8","topicId":"stock-management","front":"Anchor loop polarity?","back":"Reinforcing (all positive links) but it just replaces losses."},{"id":"dl-1","topicId":"delays-smoothing","front":"First-order material delay equation?","back":"Outflow = Stock / Delay time; steady state Stock = Inflow x delay."},{"id":"dl-2","topicId":"delays-smoothing","front":"Exponential smoothing equation?","back":"Smoothed += DT x (Input - Smoothed)/ST."},{"id":"dl-3","topicId":"delays-smoothing","front":"Difference between material and information delay?","back":"Material delay conserves items; information delay only lags a signal."},{"id":"dl-4","topicId":"delays-smoothing","front":"Risk of delays in a balancing loop?","back":"Overshoot and oscillation."},{"id":"st-1","topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","front":"University model: desired staff?","back":"Students / desired student-to-staff ratio (10000/20 = 500)."},{"id":"st-2","topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","front":"University model: hire rate?","back":"MAX(0, Expected Quits + (Desired Staff - Staff)/AT)."},{"id":"st-3","topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","front":"University: ratio drops 20 to 10 at t = 10?","back":"Desired staff 1000; staff rises goal-seeking from 500 towards 1000 (about 980 by t = 30 with AT 5)."},{"id":"st-4","topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","front":"University: auxiliary controlling reset speed?","back":"Adjustment time."},{"id":"st-5","topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","front":"Pharma model: hire rate?","back":"MAX(0, Expected Total Attrition + (300 - Total)/AT)."},{"id":"st-6","topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","front":"Pharma: progression flows?","back":"Trainee/12 and Experienced/36 per month."},{"id":"st-7","topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","front":"Pharma: target drops to 100: hiring?","back":"Adjustment very negative, hire clipped to 0; total decays via attrition, hiring resumes near the new target."},{"id":"st-8","topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","front":"Starting in equilibrium means?","back":"Expected anchor = outflow and adjustment = 0 at t = 0."},{"id":"r0-1","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Define R0.","back":"Average secondary infections from one infectious person in a totally susceptible population."},{"id":"r0-2","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"R0 in terms of c, i, d?","back":"R0 = c x i x d."},{"id":"r0-3","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Transmission rate beta?","back":"beta = R0 / D = c x i (per day)."},{"id":"r0-4","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Force of infection lambda?","back":"lambda = beta x I / N (per day per susceptible)."},{"id":"r0-5","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Infection rate?","back":"IR = S x lambda = beta S I / N."},{"id":"r0-6","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Net reproduction number?","back":"Rn = R0 x S/N."},{"id":"r0-7","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"What happens when Rn <= 1?","back":"No epidemic; infectious stock falls to zero."},{"id":"r0-8","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Herd immunity threshold?","back":"HIT = 1 - 1/R0."},{"id":"r0-9","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"HIT for R0 = 2?","back":"50%."},{"id":"r0-10","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Challenge 10.1 answers?","back":"beta = 14/7 = 2/day; lambda = 2 x 0.01 = 0.02/day."},{"id":"r0-11","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Sheet 1 Q3a answers?","back":"beta = 12/7 = 1.714; lambda = 0.0343/day; HIT = 91.7%."},{"id":"r0-12","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"What is D?","back":"Recovery delay: average duration of infectiousness."},{"id":"r0-13","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"What do c and i stand for?","back":"Contact rate (contacts/person/day) and infectivity (probability of transmission per contact)."},{"id":"r0-14","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Why does infection peak when S = N/R0?","back":"Rn = 1 there; infections stop growing."},{"id":"sir-1","topicId":"sir-model","front":"SIR stock equations?","back":"dS = -beta S I/N; dI = beta S I/N - I/D; dR = I/D."},{"id":"sir-2","topicId":"sir-model","front":"SIR loops?","back":"Contagion (R); Depletion (B); Recovery (B)."},{"id":"sir-3","topicId":"sir-model","front":"What is the attack rate?","back":"Proportion of the population ever infected: (N - S)/N."},{"id":"sir-4","topicId":"sir-model","front":"SIR initial values?","back":"S = N - I0, I = I0, R = 0."},{"id":"sir-5","topicId":"sir-model","front":"SIR assumptions?","back":"Closed population, homogeneous mixing, permanent immunity, constant parameters, deterministic."},{"id":"sir-6","topicId":"sir-model","front":"Epidemic curve (R0 2, D 2, N 10000) peak?","back":"About 1537 infected at day 18, attack rate about 80%."},{"id":"sir-7","topicId":"sir-model","front":"Why does an epidemic end before S = 0?","back":"Rn falls below 1 so infections decline."},{"id":"sir-8","topicId":"sir-model","front":"Euler form of S?","back":"S(t+DT) = S(t) - DT x IR(t)."},{"id":"cm-1","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Quarantine in the model: flows?","back":"Infection to Q = IR x QF x flag; to I = IR x (1 - QF x flag); Q recovers at Q/D."},{"id":"cm-2","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Do quarantined people transmit?","back":"No: lambda uses only I."},{"id":"cm-3","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Effective R0 with quarantine?","back":"R0 x (1 - QF)."},{"id":"cm-4","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Vaccination structure?","back":"Vaccination flow = S x VF from S to V; only susceptibles are vaccinated."},{"id":"cm-5","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"VF goal seeking equation?","back":"VF changes by (VF max - VF)/AT_v starting at 0."},{"id":"cm-6","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Attack rate with vaccination?","back":"(N - S - V)/N."},{"id":"cm-7","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Explain force of infection?","back":"beta = effective contacts per infectious person; I/N chance a contact is infectious; lambda = daily infection probability per susceptible."},{"id":"cm-8","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Why a flag for quarantine?","back":"To switch the countermeasure on/off for experiments (0 or 1)."},{"id":"cm-9","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Which lever is stronger, vaccination or quarantine?","back":"Vaccination: reduces S and Rn; quarantine only scales R0 by (1 - QF)."},{"id":"cm-10","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"QF = 0.08 result (R0 2, D 2)?","back":"Peak falls from 1537 to about 1153; attack rate 79.7% to 74.7%."},{"id":"cm-11","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Sheet 2 2c grid: biggest circle?","back":"QF 0 and VF 0 (attack rate about 80%); smallest at QF 0.15, VF 0.15 (about 0.5%)."},{"id":"cm-12","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Conservation check with Q and V?","back":"S + I + Q + R + V = N."},{"id":"tp-1","topicId":"threshold-policies","front":"Condition for infectious stock to rise?","back":"Infection inflow > recovery outflow: c i S/N > 1/d, i.e. R0 S/N > 1."},{"id":"tp-2","topicId":"threshold-policies","front":"Equilibrium condition of I?","back":"c i S/N = 1/d."},{"id":"tp-3","topicId":"threshold-policies","front":"Policies that reduce the inflow?","back":"Distancing/masks (c, i), antivirals (i), vaccination (S)."},{"id":"tp-4","topicId":"threshold-policies","front":"Policy that increases the outflow?","back":"Test-trace-isolate / quarantine (reduces d)."},{"id":"tp-5","topicId":"threshold-policies","front":"Policy parameter for vaccination?","back":"Reduces S."},{"id":"sa-1","topicId":"sensitivity-analysis","front":"What is sensitivity analysis?","back":"Rerunning the model over a range of parameter values and recording an outcome."},{"id":"sa-2","topicId":"sensitivity-analysis","front":"Attack rate vs QF shape (R0 2)?","back":"Falls slowly then collapses near QF = 0.5 where R0(1 - QF) = 1."},{"id":"ch-1","topicId":"cohort-models","front":"How to add young and elderly cohorts?","back":"Subscript stocks and flows by cohort, cohort-specific parameters, couple through a contact matrix."},{"id":"ch-2","topicId":"cohort-models","front":"Cohort force of infection?","back":"lambda_j = i x sum_k c_jk I_k / N_k."},{"id":"ch-3","topicId":"cohort-models","front":"Why use cohorts?","back":"Heterogeneous contacts and risk; test targeted policies."},{"id":"se-1","topicId":"seir-exploratory","front":"What is the Exposed stock?","back":"Infected but not yet infectious (latent period)."},{"id":"se-2","topicId":"seir-exploratory","front":"SEIR equation for E?","back":"dE/dt = beta S I/N - E/L."},{"id":"se-3","topicId":"seir-exploratory","front":"Challenge 10.3: streams?","back":"Clinical (pre-clinical then clinical, 60%) and sub-clinical (40%, half as infectious)."},{"id":"se-4","topicId":"seir-exploratory","front":"Which compartments feed the force of infection?","back":"Pre-clinical, clinical, and sub-clinical (times multiplier)."},{"id":"se-5","topicId":"seir-exploratory","front":"Challenge 10.3 R0?","back":"0.6 x (0.6 x 5 + 0.4 x 0.5 x 5) = 2.4."},{"id":"se-6","topicId":"seir-exploratory","front":"Latent effect vs SIR?","back":"Delays the peak and slows early growth; same R0."},{"id":"dv-1","topicId":"disease-model-variants","front":"SIS model?","back":"Recovered return to S; endemic equilibrium I* = N(1 - 1/R0)."},{"id":"dv-2","topicId":"disease-model-variants","front":"SIRS?","back":"Immunity wanes: R returns to S."},{"id":"dv-3","topicId":"disease-model-variants","front":"Limitations of compartment models?","back":"Homogeneous mixing, closed population, constant parameters, deterministic, aggregated."},{"id":"it-1","topicId":"implementation-tools","front":"Vensim vs Python?","back":"Vensim: diagram-based SD tool; Python: coded time loop, easy sweeps."},{"id":"bm-1","topicId":"bg-modelling-process","front":"Verification vs validation?","back":"Verification: built right; validation: right model for the purpose."},{"id":"bm-2","topicId":"bg-modelling-process","front":"Behaviour modes of SD?","back":"Exponential growth, goal seeking, S-shaped growth, oscillation, overshoot and collapse."},{"id":"bm-3","topicId":"bg-modelling-process","front":"Endogenous vs exogenous?","back":"Endogenous: generated inside the boundary by feedback; exogenous: external input."},{"id":"ab-1","topicId":"bg-stochastic-abm","front":"Deterministic vs stochastic?","back":"Deterministic: same output every run; stochastic: random, many runs."},{"id":"ab-2","topicId":"bg-stochastic-abm","front":"Agent-based model?","back":"Simulates individual agents with rules and interactions; emergent behaviour."},{"id":"ab-3","topicId":"bg-stochastic-abm","front":"Stochastic effect with I0 = 1?","back":"The outbreak can die out by chance even if R0 > 1."},{"id":"x-eu-1","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Sheet 1 Q4: why does Euler with DT = 10 fail?","back":"DT equals the whole sawtooth period, so every step uses net = -50: stock heads to -900 instead of oscillating around 100."},{"id":"x-eu-2","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Sheet 1 Q4b: Euler error over 0-5 with DT = 1?","back":"Net values -50, -30, -10, 10, 30 give S(5) = 50 vs true 100: underestimates (net rising)."},{"id":"x-eu-3","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Sheet 2 Q1b: Euler with DT = 0.5 over 0-2.5?","back":"Gives 175 vs true 162.5: overestimates by 12.5 (net falling 50 to 0)."},{"id":"x-eu-4","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Sheet 2 Q1: when does the stock cross zero?","back":"At t = 7 (falling 50 per day from 100 at t = 5); it is allowed to go negative."},{"id":"x-eu-5","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Sheet 1 Q4: why does the stock repeat every 10 minutes?","back":"Mean outflow (50) equals total inflow (50), so each 10-minute cycle has zero net area."},{"id":"x-eu-6","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"What goes on the stock-and-flow drawing for a chart question?","back":"Stock, valves for every inflow and outflow with clouds, plus a Net Flow auxiliary linked from each flow.","hook":"Net flow is drawn"},{"id":"x-eu-7","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Exam format of the graphical integration question?","back":"12 marks (model, net flow, stock plot with modes) + 5 (Euler and assumptions, over/under) + 8 (Euler at a large DT)."},{"id":"x-eu-8","topicId":"euler-integration","front":"Two assumptions of Euler to quote?","back":"Flow constant over each DT (equals its start value); DT small enough for that to be a good approximation."},{"id":"x-bf-1","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"Sheet 1 Q2a: three flows to describe?","back":"Fractional increase, fractional decrease, adjustment to a goal (equation, loop type, example, shape)."},{"id":"x-bf-2","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"Sheet 2 Q2a: how does G behave if G* = max decrease fraction?","back":"G starts low and rises by (G* - G)/AT: decay of S starts slowly then accelerates."},{"id":"x-bf-3","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"Expected Enrolments structure (Sheet 2 Q4a)?","back":"Stock Expected Enrolments changing by (Enrolments - Expected)/Time to Perceive: first-order smoothing, balancing loop."},{"id":"x-bf-4","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","front":"Time for adjustment to a goal to close 95% of a gap?","back":"About three adjustment times (63% after one)."},{"id":"x-fp-1","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Sheet 1 Q1c: name the three balancing loops.","back":"Work harder (study hours), cut time per assignment, deadline extension (time to submit)."},{"id":"x-fp-2","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Sheet 1 Q1c: name the two reinforcing side-effect loops.","back":"Rework (via less time per assignment) and fatigue (via study hours)."},{"id":"x-fp-3","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Sheet 2 Q3b: three policies against rising Work Pressure?","back":"Reduce Time per Claim, add Temporary Staff, lengthen the Workweek."},{"id":"x-fp-4","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Sheet 2 Q3b: three side effects?","back":"Less time per claim raises reworked claims; temp staff raise time per claim; longer workweek raises fatigue, which lowers completion."},{"id":"x-fp-5","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Which policy loop has three negative links?","back":"Cutting time per task: Pressure to Time (-), Time to Completed (-), Completed to Backlog (-)."},{"id":"x-fp-6","topicId":"feedback-polarity","front":"Why is Amount of Time to Submit a separate loop?","back":"It acts on Work Pressure directly (pressure up, time up, pressure down): one negative link, balancing."},{"id":"x-sm-1","topicId":"stock-management","front":"Stock management steady-state error without the anchor?","back":"Stock settles below the goal where (Goal - Stock)/AT equals the outflow."},{"id":"x-sm-2","topicId":"stock-management","front":"Sheet 2 Q4d: what happens when the target drops from 300 to 100?","back":"Adjustment very negative, MAX clips hiring to 0, total falls by attrition only, then hiring resumes near 100."},{"id":"x-sm-3","topicId":"stock-management","front":"Why can staff not fall faster than attrition?","back":"Hire rate cannot go below zero (no firing in the model), so only quits reduce the stock."},{"id":"x-sm-4","topicId":"stock-management","front":"Sheet 2 Q4c: attrition and delay values?","back":"Trainee 5%, Experienced 3%, Senior 1%; progression delays 12 months (T to E) and 36 months (E to S); target 300."},{"id":"x-sm-5","topicId":"stock-management","front":"Sheet 2 Q4c: is 100 in each grade a true equilibrium?","back":"No: only the total (hire = total attrition) is steady; the split drifts (state your assumption)."},{"id":"x-r0-1","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Notes: R0 in terms of c, i, d?","back":"R0 = c x i x d; beta = c x i."},{"id":"x-r0-2","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Equilibrium condition of the infectious stock (lecture)?","back":"dI/dt = 0 gives c i S/N = 1/d."},{"id":"x-r0-3","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Derive R0 > 1 from inflow > outflow.","back":"c i (S/N) I > I/d; with S = N: c i d > 1."},{"id":"x-r0-4","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Rn definition (lecture)?","back":"Average secondary cases per infectious person when some people are already immune; Rn = R0 x S/N."},{"id":"x-r0-5","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Challenge 10.1: N = 100,000, I = 1000, R0 = 14, D = 7?","back":"beta = 2 per day; lambda = 0.02 per day."},{"id":"x-r0-6","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Shape of HIT against R0?","back":"Zero at R0 = 1, steep rise, then flattening towards 100%."},{"id":"x-r0-7","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Lecture R0 and herd-immunity ranges: measles, influenza?","back":"Measles R0 12-18, herd immunity 83-94%; influenza R0 2-4, 50-75%."},{"id":"x-r0-8","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","front":"Lecture target for vaccination coverage?","back":"Above the HIT, so substantial outbreaks are unlikely."},{"id":"x-cm-1","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Sheet 1 Q3b: model parameters?","back":"R0 = 2, D = 2 days, N = 10,000, I0 = 1, QF = 0.08, flag 0/1; beta = 1."},{"id":"x-cm-2","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Sheet 2 Q2b: model parameters?","back":"20% of infections to Q, 80% to I; VF 0 goal-seeking to 0.15; R0 = 2, D = 2, N = 10,000."},{"id":"x-cm-3","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Quarantine in the policy table: which parameter and flow?","back":"d (time infectious and circulating): increases the outflow."},{"id":"x-cm-4","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Vaccination in the policy table?","back":"Reduces S, which reduces the inflow."},{"id":"x-cm-5","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Antivirals and masks in the policy table?","back":"Antivirals reduce i (inflow); masks/distancing reduce c and i (inflow)."},{"id":"x-cm-6","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Attack rate at QF 0.2, no vaccination (R0 2)?","back":"About 64%."},{"id":"x-cm-7","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Attack rate with QF 0.2 and VF to 0.15?","back":"About 0.4%."},{"id":"x-cm-8","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Sheet 2 Q2c: circle size means?","back":"Final attack rate at that combination of QF (x) and final VF (y)."},{"id":"x-cm-9","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","front":"Final-size attack rate for R0 = 2?","back":"About 79.7% (solve z = 1 - e^(-R0 z))."},{"id":"x-ch-1","topicId":"cohort-models","front":"Sheet 1 Q3c: five marks for what?","back":"Subscripted cohort stocks and flows, cohort parameters, contact matrix coupling, same structure, why."}],"methods":[{"id":"m-net-stock","topicId":"euler-integration","title":"Net flow and stock from a flow chart (graphical integration)","when":"Given a chart of inflows and outflows and an initial stock","mnemonic":"Lines to equations, subtract, find zeros, add areas","steps":["1. Write each flow segment as an equation (slope x t + intercept) and check the corner values.","2. Net flow = inflows - outflows; calculate it at every corner.","3. Find where the net flow crosses zero (stock turning points).","4. For each interval compute the area under the net flow (triangles 0.5 x base x height, rectangles base x height), signed.","5. Add the areas to the initial stock to get the stock at each corner; plot it.","6. Label each interval with a behaviour mode (rising/falling, at an increasing/decreasing/constant rate).","7. Draw the stock and flow model with a Net Flow auxiliary."],"example":{"problem":"Sheet 1 Q4: inflows 30 and 20, outflow sawtooth 100 to 0 (t = 5) to 100 (t = 10); stock starts at 100.","solution":["Outflow = 100 - 20t on [0,5]; net = 50 - outflow = -50 + 20t.","Net: -50 at 0, 0 at 2.5, +50 at 5, 0 at 7.5, -50 at 10 (repeats).","Areas: 0.5 x 2.5 x 50 = 62.5 per triangle.","Stock: 100, 37.5 (t 2.5), 100 (5), 162.5 (7.5), 100 (10), then the same for 10-20.","Modes: 0-2.5 falls at a decreasing rate; 2.5-5 rises at an increasing rate; 5-7.5 rises at a decreasing rate; 7.5-10 falls at an increasing rate."]}},{"id":"m-euler-calc","topicId":"euler-integration","title":"Compute a stock with Euler at a given DT","when":"Asked to calculate the stock using Euler's equation for DT = ...","mnemonic":"Start value, read at the START, times DT, add","steps":["1. Write Stock(t+DT) = Stock(t) + DT x Net(t).","2. List the times 0, DT, 2 DT, ... up to the end.","3. Read the net flow at each START time from the chart (compute both inflows and outflows).","4. Multiply by DT and add to the previous stock; tabulate.","5. Compare with the exact stock and comment on the error."],"example":{"problem":"Sheet 2 Q1c: net flow +50 at t = 0, -50 at 5 and 10, +50 at 15 and 20; stock(0) = 100; DT = 5.","solution":["S(5) = 100 + 5 x 50 = 350.","S(10) = 350 + 5 x (-50) = 100.","S(15) = 100 + 5 x (-50) = -150.","S(20) = -150 + 5 x 50 = 100.","S(25) = 100 + 5 x 50 = 350.","Exact values at 5, 10, 15, 20, 25: 100, -150, -150, 100, 100: DT = 5 is too coarse."]}},{"id":"m-euler-error","topicId":"euler-integration","title":"Show over/under-estimation by Euler","when":"Under what conditions does Euler over/underestimate? support with the chart","mnemonic":"Rising net = under, falling net = over","steps":["1. State: Euler uses the net flow at the start of each step.","2. Decide whether the net flow is increasing (under) or decreasing (over) over the interval.","3. Compute the exact change (area under the net flow).","4. Compute the Euler value for the DT asked.","5. Subtract and state the sign of the error; mention smaller DT reduces it."],"example":{"problem":"Sheet 2 Q1b: net = 50 - 20t on 0-2.5, DT = 0.5, stock(0) = 100.","solution":["Net decreasing from 50 to 0, so Euler overestimates.","Exact change = 0.5 x 2.5 x 50 = 62.5, stock 162.5.","Net at 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2 = 50, 40, 30, 20, 10; DT x sum = 0.5 x 150 = 75.","Euler stock = 175 (path 125, 145, 160, 170, 175).","Error = +12.5 (overestimate)."]}},{"id":"m-beta-lambda","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","title":"Compute beta, lambda and infection rate","when":"Given N, % infectious, R0 and D","mnemonic":"R0 over D, times I over N","steps":["1. I = percentage x N.","2. beta = R0 / D (per day).","3. lambda = beta x I / N.","4. Infection rate = S x lambda, with S = N - I (if asked).","5. Give units: per day."],"example":{"problem":"Sheet 1 Q3a: N = 10,000, 2% infectious, R0 = 12, D = 7.","solution":["I = 200.","beta = 12/7 = 1.714 per day.","lambda = 1.714 x 200/10000 = 0.0343 per day.","Infection rate = 9800 x 0.0343 = 336 people per day."]}},{"id":"m-hit-rn","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","title":"Herd immunity threshold and Rn","when":"Asked for HIT, vaccination target or whether an epidemic can grow","mnemonic":"One minus one over R0","steps":["1. HIT = 1 - 1/R0.","2. People to vaccinate = HIT x N.","3. Rn = R0 x S/N for the current susceptible fraction.","4. Rn > 1 grows; Rn < 1 declines.","5. Peak occurs when S = N/R0."],"example":{"problem":"R0 = 12, N = 10,000; 70% already immune. Can an epidemic grow?","solution":["HIT = 1 - 1/12 = 0.9167: need 9,167 immune.","S/N = 0.30 so Rn = 12 x 0.30 = 3.6 > 1: yes it can grow.","Extra immune needed: 91.67% - 70% = 21.67%, i.e. 2,167 more people."]}},{"id":"m-sir-equations","topicId":"sir-model","title":"Write the SIR equations and run an Euler step","when":"Design an SIR model or simulate first steps","mnemonic":"beta, lambda, IR, RR, then update all stocks together","steps":["1. Set initial values S = N - I0, I = I0, R = 0.","2. beta = R0/D.","3. lambda = beta x I/N; IR = S x lambda; RR = I/D.","4. S += DT x (-IR); I += DT x (IR - RR); R += DT x RR (use old values for all).","5. Check S + I + R = N.","6. Attack rate = (N - S)/N."],"example":{"problem":"N = 1000, I0 = 10, R0 = 3, D = 4, DT = 1: first step.","solution":["beta = 0.75; lambda = 0.75 x 10/1000 = 0.0075.","IR = 990 x 0.0075 = 7.425; RR = 10/4 = 2.5.","S = 982.575; I = 10 + 7.425 - 2.5 = 14.925; R = 2.5.","Sum = 1000."]}},{"id":"m-quarantine","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","title":"Add quarantine with a control flag","when":"Extend SIR with a quarantine fraction","mnemonic":"Split the infection flow; Q recovers, Q never infects","steps":["1. Add stock Q (initial 0).","2. Effective fraction = QF x Flag.","3. Infection to Q = IR x QF x Flag; infection to I = IR x (1 - QF x Flag).","4. Recovery I = I/D; Recovery Q = Q/D; R receives both.","5. lambda = beta x I/N (exclude Q).","6. Add Attack Rate = (N - S)/N and label loops (contagion R, depletion B, recoveries B)."],"example":{"problem":"N = 10,000, I0 = 1, R0 = 2, D = 2, QF = 0.08, flag 1: first-step values.","solution":["beta = 1; lambda = 1 x 1/10000 = 0.0001; IR = 9999 x 0.0001 = 0.9999.","To Q = 0.9999 x 0.08 = 0.080; to I = 0.9199.","Recovery I = 1/2 = 0.5; Recovery Q = 0.","Effective R0 = 2 x 0.92 = 1.84."]}},{"id":"m-vaccination","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","title":"Add a vaccination stock with goal-seeking fraction","when":"Vaccinated stock where only susceptibles move through a fractional decrease flow","mnemonic":"S times VF drains to V; VF chases its maximum","steps":["1. Add stock V (initial 0) and stock VF (initial 0).","2. Vaccination = S x VF (outflow of S, inflow of V).","3. VF flow = (VF max - VF)/AT_v.","4. S changes by -IR - Vaccination.","5. Attack rate = (N - S - V)/N.","6. Show loops: vaccination (B), VF goal seeking (B) plus the SIR loops."],"example":{"problem":"VF max = 0.15, AT_v = 10 days: VF values?","solution":["VF(t) = 0.15 x (1 - e^(-t/10)).","Day 5: 0.059; day 10: 0.095; day 20: 0.130; day 30: 0.143 (simulated values).","Vaccination at day 10 with S = 5739: about 545 people per day."]}},{"id":"m-stock-mgmt","topicId":"stock-management","title":"Design a stock management structure","when":"Design staff, inventory or capacity control with a target","mnemonic":"Desired, Gap, Adjustment, Anchor, MAX","steps":["1. Identify the stock, its outflow (fraction x stock) and the desired stock (from its driver).","2. Adjustment = (Desired - Stock)/AT.","3. Anchor = Expected outflow (smoothing stock initialised at the outflow).","4. Inflow = MAX(0, Anchor + Adjustment).","5. Initialise at equilibrium: Stock = Desired, Expected = Outflow.","6. List loops: adjustment B, outflow B, anchor R; sketch response to a step in the desired value."],"example":{"problem":"Staff 500, quit fraction 0.1, desired 1000, AT = 5: hire rate right after the step.","solution":["Quits = 50; anchor = 50.","Adjustment = (1000 - 500)/5 = 100.","Hire = MAX(0, 50 + 100) = 150 per time unit.","Quits at 1000 staff = 100, so hires fall towards 100."]}},{"id":"m-equilibrium-check","topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","title":"Check and set equilibrium initial values","when":"Model \"should start in equilibrium\"","mnemonic":"Inflow = outflow at t = 0","steps":["1. Set the stock to the given value.","2. Compute the outflow from its fraction.","3. Set the anchor stock = that outflow.","4. Set the desired value = the stock so the adjustment is zero.","5. Verify net flow = 0 for the stock."],"example":{"problem":"University: staff 500, students 10,000, ratio 20, quit fraction 0.1.","solution":["Desired = 10000/20 = 500 = staff: adjustment 0.","Quits = 0.1 x 500 = 50; Expected Quits(0) = 50; hire = 50.","Net = 50 - 50 = 0: equilibrium. Students: recruitment fraction = graduation fraction (0.25 each) gives 2500 in and out."]}},{"id":"m-polarity","topicId":"feedback-polarity","title":"Trace link and loop polarity","when":"Asked to confirm polarity by tracing an increasing backlog","mnemonic":"Push the stock up and walk round","steps":["1. Write the loop as a cycle and sign every link.","2. Increase the first variable and write up/down at each variable.","3. Compare the final effect on the start variable.","4. Same direction (even negatives): reinforcing; opposite (odd): balancing.","5. Name and label the loop on the diagram."],"example":{"problem":"Assignment model: loop Backlog, Work Pressure, Time Per Assignment, Completed, Backlog.","solution":["Backlog up -> Work Pressure up (+).","Work Pressure up -> Time Per Assignment down (-).","Time Per Assignment down -> Completed up (-).","Completed up -> Backlog down (-).","Result opposite to start; three negatives: balancing (B2)."]}},{"id":"m-backlog-model","topicId":"workload-backlog-models","title":"Build a backlog stock-flow model from a description","when":"Construct model from numbered bullet description with polarities","mnemonic":"Stock first, then each numbered sentence becomes one link","steps":["1. Draw the stock with inflows and outflows.","2. Add each variable named in the description.","3. Turn every sentence into an arrow with sign (more X leads to less Y = -).","4. Flow to stock links: inflow +, outflow -.","5. Find loops starting at the backlog; sign and trace each; label policy loops B and side-effect loops R.","6. Check every variable in the text appears."],"example":{"problem":"Claims model: how many loops?","solution":["Policy loops (B): time per claim (3 negatives), temp staff (1), workweek (1).","Side-effect loops (R): rework (2 negatives), temp staff raising time per claim (2), fatigue (2)."]}},{"id":"m-fractional-flows","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","title":"Compute fractional increase, decrease and goal seeking by Euler","when":"Describe flows with examples and numbers","mnemonic":"Fraction x stock, or gap over AT","steps":["1. Choose the structure: growth (g x S), decay (f x S), goal (gap/AT).","2. Write the equation and loop type.","3. Give a real example.","4. Step by Euler with DT = 1 and tabulate.","5. State the behaviour shape."],"example":{"problem":"Goal 100, stock 20, AT 4, DT 1.","solution":["Flow = (100 - 20)/4 = 20, stock 40.","Flow = 15, stock 55.","Flow = 11.25, stock 66.25.","Flow = 8.44, stock 74.7: exponential approach."]}},{"id":"m-cohort","topicId":"cohort-models","title":"Modify a model for cohorts","when":"Represent young and elderly populations","mnemonic":"Subscript, parameterise, couple","steps":["1. Subscript stocks and flows by cohort.","2. Give cohort-specific sizes, D, QF, VF, mortality.","3. Add contact matrix c_jk.","4. lambda_j = i x sum_k c_jk I_k / N_k.","5. Report outcomes per cohort and total."],"example":{"problem":"Young contact 10/day (8 with young, 2 with elderly); i = 0.05; I_y/N_y = 0.02; I_e/N_e = 0.01.","solution":["lambda_young = 0.05 x (8 x 0.02 + 2 x 0.01) = 0.05 x 0.18 = 0.009 per day."]}},{"id":"m-seir","topicId":"seir-exploratory","title":"Extend SIR to SEIR with streams","when":"Add latent stock or clinical and sub-clinical streams","mnemonic":"Infection feeds E, E splits by CF","steps":["1. Add E; infection rate feeds E.","2. E outflow = E / latent time, split CF to clinical, 1 - CF to sub-clinical.","3. Add chain pre-clinical then clinical with their times.","4. lambda sums all infectious compartments (sub-clinical times multiplier).","5. R0 = c i [CF x total clinical time + (1 - CF) x multiplier x sub-clinical time]."],"example":{"problem":"Challenge 10.3 R0.","solution":["c i = 10 x 0.06 = 0.6.","Clinical total time = 2.1 + 2.9 = 5.","R0 = 0.6 x (0.6 x 5 + 0.4 x 0.5 x 5) = 0.6 x (3 + 1) = 2.4."]}},{"id":"m-compare-template","topicId":"feedback-polarity","title":"Answer template: define / describe / discuss","when":"Essay-style parts (advantages of feedback, best course of action, comment on real-world use)","mnemonic":"Define, mechanism, example, consequence","steps":["1. Define the term precisely.","2. Give the mechanism (equation or loop).","3. Give one concrete example from the question.","4. State the behaviour or consequence.","5. Add a limitation or trade-off if marks allow."],"example":{"problem":"Advantages of feedback structures in stock and flow models (5 marks).","solution":["Define feedback loop; R and B types (2).","Behaviour generated endogenously: growth, goal seeking, oscillation (1).","Policy response and testing of interventions (1).","Reveals side effects and shifting dominance (1)."]}}],"practice":[{"id":"s1-q1a","topicId":"feedback-polarity","source":"Problem Sheet 1 Q1(a)","marks":5,"question":"Define a feedback loop, and describe the two different types of feedback. Summarise the advantages of adding feedback structures to a stock and flow model.","outline":["Feedback loop: closed chain of causal links where a variable ultimately influences itself","Reinforcing (positive): amplifies change, exponential growth or collapse, even number of negative links","Balancing (negative): counteracts change, goal seeking, odd number of negative links","Example of each (contagion R; recovery or thermostat B)","Advantages: endogenous behaviour, policy response, explains growth/oscillation, tests policies, shows side effects"]},{"id":"s1-q1b","topicId":"workload-backlog-models","source":"Problem Sheet 1 Q1(b)","marks":12,"question":"Construct a stock and flow model of the Assignment Backlog (inflows Allocated and Rework, outflow Completed) from the description of Work Pressure, Time Per Assignment, Weekly Study Hours, Amount of Time to Submit and Fatigue, clearly showing link polarities.","outline":["Stock Assignment Backlog with inflows Allocated, Rework and outflow Completed with clouds","Backlog to Work Pressure +","Work Pressure to Time Per Assignment -, to Weekly Study Hours +, to Amount of Time to Submit +","Time Per Assignment to Rework - and to Completed -","Weekly Study Hours to Completed + and to Fatigue +","Amount of Time to Submit to Work Pressure -; Fatigue to Completed -","Flow to stock links + (inflows) and - (outflow); all variables included"]},{"id":"s1-q1c","topicId":"workload-backlog-models","source":"Problem Sheet 1 Q1(c)","marks":8,"question":"Show (and name) the three negative feedback loops for managing the backlog and the two positive loops that are side effects; confirm polarity by tracing an increasing backlog.","outline":["B1 Work harder: backlog +, pressure +, study hours +, completed up, backlog down","B2 Cut time per assignment: pressure up, time per assignment down, completed up, backlog down (3 negatives)","B3 Deadline extension: pressure up, time to submit up, pressure down","R1 Rework: time per assignment down, rework up, backlog up (2 negatives)","R2 Fatigue: study hours up, fatigue up, completed down, backlog up (2 negatives)","Each loop traced and labelled B or R"]},{"id":"s1-q2a","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","source":"Problem Sheet 1 Q2(a)","marks":6,"question":"Describe, showing relevant examples, the flows: Fractional Increase Rate, Fractional Decrease Rate, Adjustment to a Goal.","outline":["Fractional increase: inflow = stock x fraction, reinforcing, exponential growth, example births / interest","Fractional decrease: outflow = stock x fraction (stock/lifetime), balancing, exponential decay, example quits / recovery","Adjustment to a goal: (goal - stock)/AT, balancing, goal seeking, example thermostat / workforce","Equations and loop types for each","Shape of behaviour for each"]},{"id":"s1-q2b","topicId":"stock-management","source":"Problem Sheet 1 Q2(b)","marks":7,"question":"Explain how adjustment to a goal can be extended to the stock management structure, describing the anchor and the adjustment; show the delay structure used to construct the anchor.","outline":["Adjustment to a goal alone leaves a steady-state error when there is an outflow","Stock management: inflow = MAX(0, anchor + adjustment)","Anchor = expected outflow, replaces what is lost, holds stock when on target","Adjustment = (desired - stock)/AT, closes the gap","Anchor built as first-order smoothing / information delay: expected outflow changes by (outflow - expected)/smoothing time","Diagram of the stock, flows, expected outflow stock and links"]},{"id":"s1-q2c","topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","source":"Problem Sheet 1 Q2(c)","marks":12,"question":"Design a stock and flow model (with equations) of staff recruitment in a University as a stock management structure; staff 500, students 10,000 in equilibrium, desired ratio 20 drops to 10 at time 10. Plot staff to time 30 and name the auxiliary with a big influence on reset speed.","outline":["Students stock with recruitment and graduation fractions equal (equilibrium)","Desired Staff = Students / ratio (500, then 1000); ratio STEP at t = 10","Staff stock: hire - quits; Quits = Staff x quit fraction","Hire = MAX(0, expected quits + (desired - staff)/AT) with anchor delay equation","Initial values in equilibrium (Staff 500, expected quits = quits)","Feedback loops: adjustment B, quits B, anchor R","Plot: flat to t = 10, then goal-seeking rise towards 1000","Adjustment time is the key auxiliary"]},{"id":"s1-q3a","topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","source":"Problem Sheet 1 Q3(a)","marks":5,"question":"Town of 10,000, 2% infectious with measles, R0 = 12, D = 7 days. Calculate the force of infection and the herd immunity threshold.","outline":["I = 200","beta = R0/D = 12/7 = 1.714 per day","lambda = beta x I/N = 0.0343 per day","HIT = 1 - 1/12 = 0.9167 = 91.7%"]},{"id":"s1-q3b","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","source":"Problem Sheet 1 Q3(b)","marks":15,"question":"Design a stock and flow model (equations and feedbacks) of influenza spread: SIR core, R0 = 2, recovery delay 2 days, N = 10,000 with one infected; quarantine fraction QF = 0.08 for infected, quarantined recover at the same rate, quarantine switched by a flag; explain the force of infection equation.","outline":["Stocks S, I, Q, R; initial 9999, 1, 0, 0 (state whether quarantine splits the infection flow, as in the lecture, or leaves I)","beta = R0/D = 1; lambda = beta I/N; IR = S lambda","Quarantine flow = IR x QF x flag; infection to I = IR x (1 - QF x flag)","Recovery I/D and Q/D into R","Quarantined do not enter the force of infection","Flag 0/1, QF = 0.08","Loops: contagion R; depletion B; recovery B (x2)","Explanation: beta contacts, I/N probability, lambda per-susceptible daily risk","Optional attack rate / expected behaviour"]},{"id":"s1-q3c","topicId":"cohort-models","source":"Problem Sheet 1 Q3(c)","marks":5,"question":"Show how the model would need to be modified to represent different cohorts, for example young and elderly.","outline":["Subscript stocks and flows by cohort","Cohort-specific parameters (size, D, QF, VF, mortality)","Contact matrix coupling cohorts: lambda_j = i x sum c_jk I_k/N_k","Same structure per cohort; compare outcomes","Reason: heterogeneous contacts and risk"]},{"id":"s1-q4a","topicId":"euler-integration","source":"Problem Sheet 1 Q4(a)","marks":12,"question":"Three flows: sawtooth outflow 100 to 0 at t = 5 to 100 at t = 10 (repeating), constant inflows 30 and 20. Draw the stock and flow model (with net flow), calculate the net flow, graphically integrate the stock (starts at 100) and explain the behaviour modes.","outline":["Diagram: stock, two inflows, outflow, net flow auxiliary","Net = 50 - outflow: -50 + 20t on 0-5, 150 - 20t on 5-10","Zero crossings at 2.5, 7.5, 12.5, 17.5","Stock: 100, 37.5, 100, 162.5, 100, repeated","Modes per interval (falling at a decreasing rate etc.)","Mean outflow = mean inflow so oscillates around 100"]},{"id":"s1-q4b","topicId":"euler-integration","source":"Problem Sheet 1 Q4(b)","marks":5,"question":"Describe Euler's method and its assumptions. When does it underestimate a stock? Support with the net flow over 0-5.","outline":["Stock(t+DT) = Stock(t) + DT x net flow(t)","Assumptions: flow constant over step, small DT","Underestimates when net flow is increasing over the step","Net flow rises from -50 to +50 over 0-5","DT = 5 gives -150 vs true 100; DT = 1 gives 50 vs 100"]},{"id":"s1-q4c","topicId":"euler-integration","source":"Problem Sheet 1 Q4(c)","marks":8,"question":"Using Euler's equation with DT = 10, calculate the stock based on the flows in part (a).","outline":["Net(0) = 50 - 100 = -50; Net(10) = -50","S(10) = 100 + 10(-50) = -400","S(20) = -400 + 10(-50) = -900","True stock is 100 at 10 and 20: DT too large","Comment on error"]},{"id":"s2-q1a","topicId":"euler-integration","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q1(a)","marks":12,"question":"Inflow (100 to 0 at t = 5, 0 until 10, up to 100 at 15, 100 until 20, down to 0 at 25), outflow constant 50. Draw the stock and flow model, calculate the net flow, graphically integrate the stock from 100 and explain the behaviour modes.","outline":["Diagram with net flow","Net = inflow - 50: 50 - 20t, -50, -50 + 20(t - 10), +50, 50 - 20(t - 20)","Zero crossings 2.5, 12.5, 22.5","Stock: 162.5 (2.5), 100 (5), -150 (10), -212.5 (12.5), -150 (15), 100 (20), 162.5 (22.5), 100 (25)","Mode per interval: rising at decreasing rate, falling at increasing rate, linear fall, ...","Negative stock allowed"]},{"id":"s2-q1b","topicId":"euler-integration","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q1(b)","marks":5,"question":"Describe Euler's method and its assumptions. When does it overestimate the stock? Support with the net flow over 0-2.5 with DT = 0.5.","outline":["Equation and assumptions","Overestimates when the net flow is decreasing","Net 50, 40, 30, 20, 10 at 0 ... 2","Euler stock 175 vs true 162.5, error +12.5"]},{"id":"s2-q1c","topicId":"euler-integration","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q1(c)","marks":8,"question":"Using Euler's equation with DT = 5, calculate the stock for the flows in part (a).","outline":["Net at 0, 5, 10, 15, 20 = 50, -50, -50, 50, 50","Stock 350, 100, -150, 100, 350 at 5 ... 25","Compare with exact 100, -150, -150, 100, 100"]},{"id":"s2-q2a","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q2(a)","marks":8,"question":"For a stock S show how its fractional decrease flow can allow a varying decrease fraction G, where G is an adjustment to a goal G* with constant AT. Comment on real-world use.","outline":["Outflow = S x G","G is a stock: G + DT x (G* - G)/AT with initial value","Goal-seeking balancing loop for G, B loop S to outflow","Behaviour: slow start then faster decay then levels","Real-world: gradual vaccination, quitting, policy phase-in"]},{"id":"s2-q2b","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q2(b)","marks":12,"question":"Extend the SIR model with a quarantine stock (20% of the infection flow; 80% to infected) and a vaccinated stock (fractional decrease of susceptibles, vaccination fraction from 0 to 0.15 by goal seeking); R0 = 2, N = 10,000, one infected, quarantined do not infect, include attack rate; give all equations and feedbacks.","outline":["Stocks S, I, Q, R, V, VF; initial values","beta = R0/D; lambda = beta I/N; IR = S lambda","To Q = 0.2 IR; to I = 0.8 IR; recoveries I/D, Q/D","Vaccination = S x VF; VF goal seeking to 0.15","Attack rate = (N - S - V)/N","Loops: contagion R; depletion, recovery, vaccination, VF goal seeking B"]},{"id":"s2-q2c","topicId":"sir-countermeasures","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q2(c)","marks":5,"question":"Sketch and explain the relationship between quarantine fraction (0, 0.05, 0.10, 0.15) and final vaccination fraction (0, 0.05, 0.10, 0.15) using circle size for the final attack rate.","outline":["Grid with circles","Largest at QF 0, VF 0 (about 80%), smallest at QF 0.15, VF 0.15","Vaccination reduces S and Rn so it has the stronger effect","Quarantine reduces effective R0 by (1 - QF) only","Combined effect synergistic, near zero at high VF"]},{"id":"s2-q3a","topicId":"feedback-polarity","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q3(a)","marks":6,"question":"Using the Environmental Quality, Pressure to Clean Up Environment, Cleanup Effort loop, define link polarity, loop polarity and feedback and discuss the behaviour generated.","outline":["Link polarity definitions with examples from the loop","Quality to Pressure negative; Pressure to Effort +; Effort to Quality +","Loop polarity: one negative link, balancing","Feedback defined","Behaviour: goal seeking, oscillation if delays"]},{"id":"s2-q3b","topicId":"workload-backlog-models","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q3(b)","marks":12,"question":"Construct a stock and flow model of the insurance Claims Backlog with Work Pressure, Time per Claim, Temporary Staff, Workweek and Fatigue, showing link polarities and loop polarities.","outline":["Stock Claims Backlog, inflows New Claims, Reworked Claims, outflow Claims Completed","Backlog to Work Pressure +","Work Pressure to Time per Claim -, Temp Staff +, Workweek +","Time per Claim to Claims Completed -; Temp Staff +; Workweek +","Side effects: Time per Claim to Reworked Claims -; Temp Staff to Time per Claim +; Workweek to Fatigue +; Fatigue to Completed -","Loops labelled with polarity"]},{"id":"s2-q3c","topicId":"workload-backlog-models","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q3(c)","marks":7,"question":"Identify the three policy loops and three side-effect loops, and discuss the best course of action to cope with increased workload.","outline":["Policy loops B1 time per claim, B2 temp staff, B3 workweek","Side-effect loops R1 rework, R2 temp staff slows time per claim, R3 fatigue","Trace polarity for each","Discussion: temp staff main, limited overtime, avoid cutting time per claim","Link to delays and side effects"]},{"id":"s2-q4a","topicId":"basic-flow-structures","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q4(a)","marks":5,"question":"For the Students stock with Enrolments inflow and Graduations outflow, add a stock and flow structure with equations to model expected enrolments.","outline":["New stock Expected Enrolments (information delay / smoothing) with initial value equal to Enrolments","Change in Expected Enrolments = (Enrolments - Expected Enrolments)/Time to Perceive Enrolments","Balancing goal-seeking loop; link polarities shown (Enrolments +, Expected -)","Graduations = Students / Programme Duration; Students + DT x (Enrolments - Graduations)","Say how expected enrolments is used (planning staff or capacity); equations and initial values for all new variables"]},{"id":"s2-q4b","topicId":"stock-management","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q4(b)","marks":5,"question":"Explain how adjustment to a goal extends to the stock management structure and the key difference; describe the anchor and the adjustment.","outline":["Adjustment to goal: (goal - stock)/AT","Stock management: anchor + adjustment with MAX(0)","Anchor = expected outflow","Adjustment = discrepancy / AT","Key difference: anchor keeps the stock on target despite outflow; no steady-state error"]},{"id":"s2-q4c","topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q4(c)","marks":10,"question":"Create a stock and flow model with all equations of staff recruitment for a pharmaceutical company using stock management for the hire rate; Trainee, Experienced and Senior stocks, target 300, 100 in each, progression delays 12 and 36 months, attrition 5%, 3%, 1%; show all feedbacks.","outline":["Three stocks with hire into Trainee, progression Trainee/12, Experienced/36","Attrition flows at 0.05, 0.03, 0.01 x stock","Total, target 300, adjustment = (target - total)/AT","Anchor = expected total attrition (smoothed)","Hire = MAX(0, anchor + adjustment)","Feedbacks: adjustment B, attrition B, progression, anchor R","Initial values 100 each"]},{"id":"s2-q4d","topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","source":"Problem Sheet 2 Q4(d)","marks":5,"question":"The model in (c) is in steady state with 300 employees. Sketch what happens if the target drops to 100 and how the equations react.","outline":["Adjustment = (100 - 300)/AT strongly negative","MAX clips hire to zero; no firing","Total declines via attrition, exponentially slowing","Trainee stock empties first; anchor falls","Hiring resumes near the new target, total settles at 100"]}],"mocks":[{"id":"mock1","title":"Mock Paper 1","duration":120,"instructions":"Format of the real CT561 paper: 2 hours. Answer 3 questions out of 4. All questions are marked equally (25 marks each). Show all equations, working, initial values and units.","questions":[{"n":1,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":5,"topicId":"feedback-polarity","text":"Define a feedback loop and describe the two different types of feedback, with an example of each. Summarise the advantages of adding feedback structures to a stock and flow model. [5]","outline":["Feedback loop: closed chain of causal links so a variable influences itself (1)","Reinforcing: amplifies change, exponential growth or collapse, even number of negative links, e.g. contagion (1)","Balancing: counteracts change, goal seeking, odd number of negative links, e.g. recovery or thermostat (1)","Advantages: behaviour generated endogenously, policy response and testing, shows side effects (1)","Explains growth, oscillation, overshoot; shifting loop dominance (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":12,"topicId":"workload-backlog-models","text":"Construct a stock and flow model from the following description of a hospital waiting list, clearly showing the link polarities. There is a single stock (Waiting List) with two inflows (Referrals and Re-referrals) and one outflow (Treatments). (1) Waiting List is increased by the inflows and reduced by the outflow. (2) As Waiting List increases, so does Waiting Pressure. (3) An increase in Waiting Pressure leads to: a decrease in Consultation Time; an increase in Overtime; an increase in Outsourced Capacity. (4) A decrease in Consultation Time leads to an increase in Treatments and an increase in Re-referrals. (5) An increase in Overtime leads to an increase in Treatments and an increase in Burnout. (6) An increase in Burnout leads to a decrease in Treatments. (7) An increase in Outsourced Capacity leads to an increase in Treatments and an increase in Re-referrals (poor continuity of care). [12]","outline":["Stock Waiting List with clouds; inflows Referrals and Re-referrals (+), outflow Treatments (-) (3)","Waiting List to Waiting Pressure + (1)","Pressure to Consultation Time -, to Overtime +, to Outsourced Capacity + (3)","Consultation Time to Treatments - and to Re-referrals - (2)","Overtime to Treatments + and to Burnout +; Burnout to Treatments - (2)","Outsourced Capacity to Treatments + and to Re-referrals + (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"workload-backlog-models","text":"Based on your model: (i) show and name the three balancing loops used to manage the waiting list as pressure increases; (ii) show and name the three reinforcing loops that are side effects of these policies. Confirm each loop polarity by tracing an increasing Waiting List round the loop. [8]","outline":["B1 shorter consultations: list up, pressure up, consultation time DOWN, treatments UP, list down: 3 negative links, balancing","B2 overtime: pressure up, overtime up, treatments up, list down: 1 negative, balancing","B3 outsourcing: pressure up, capacity up, treatments up, list down: 1 negative, balancing","R1 re-referrals from short consultations: consultation time down, re-referrals UP, list UP: 2 negatives (pressure to time, time to re-referrals), reinforcing","R2 burnout: overtime up, burnout up, treatments DOWN, list UP: 2 negatives, reinforcing","R3 continuity: capacity up, re-referrals UP, list UP, pressure up: 0 negatives, reinforcing","Each loop named and labelled R or B, trace written out"]}]},{"n":2,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":6,"topicId":"basic-flow-structures","text":"Describe, showing relevant examples, the following flows: fractional increase rate, fractional decrease rate, adjustment to a goal. Give the equation and loop type of each. [6]","outline":["Fractional increase: inflow = Stock x g, reinforcing, exponential growth, e.g. births or early epidemic (2)","Fractional decrease: outflow = Stock x f = Stock/lifetime, balancing, exponential decay, e.g. quits or recovery (2)","Adjustment to a goal: (Goal - Stock)/AT, balancing, goal seeking (63% of the gap per AT), e.g. thermostat (2)"]},{"label":"b","marks":7,"topicId":"stock-management","text":"Explain how the adjustment to a goal structure can be extended to the stock management structure. When explaining it, describe (1) the anchor and (2) the adjustment. Show the delay structure used to construct the anchor. [7]","outline":["Adjustment to a goal alone leaves a steady-state error when the stock also has an outflow (1)","Inflow = MAX(0, Anchor + Adjustment) (1)","Anchor = expected outflow, holds the stock level when on target (1)","Adjustment = (Desired - Stock)/AT closes the gap (1)","Anchor delay: stock Expected Outflow changing by (Outflow - Expected)/smoothing time, initialised at the outflow (2)","Diagram with stock, flows, Expected Outflow stock and links (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":12,"topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","text":"A call centre has 200 agents (equilibrium). 5% of agents quit each week. Each agent handles 20 calls per week. Weekly demand is 4,000 calls and rises to 5,000 at week 10. Hiring uses the stock management structure with adjustment time AT = 8 weeks and a 2-week smoothing time for expected quits. Design the stock and flow model with all equations and initial values, show the feedback loops, calculate the hire rate at week 10 and at the new equilibrium, sketch the number of agents to week 40 and name the auxiliary with the biggest influence on how fast the model resets. [12]","outline":["Demand = 4000 + STEP(1000, 10); Desired Agents = Demand/20 = 200 then 250 (2)","Agents(0) = 200; Agents + DT x (Hire Rate - Quits); Quits = 0.05 x Agents = 10 (2)","Expected Quits stock (0) = 10; change = (Quits - Expected Quits)/2 (2)","Adjustment = (Desired - Agents)/8; Hire Rate = MAX(0, Expected Quits + Adjustment) (2)","Loops: adjustment B, quits B, anchor R (1)","Hire at week 10 = 10 + (250 - 200)/8 = 16.25 per week; new equilibrium hire = quits = 12.5 per week at 250 agents (1)","Sketch: flat at 200 then goal-seeking rise to 250 (about 210.7 at week 12, 222 at week 15, 234 at week 20, 245 at week 30, 248 at week 40) (1)","Adjustment time AT is the key auxiliary (with AT = 4 the hire at week 10 is 22.5 and the approach is faster) (1)"]}]},{"n":3,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":5,"topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","text":"A town has N = 50,000 people, 0.5% of whom are infectious with a respiratory infection with R0 = 5 and average infectious period D = 4 days. Nobody is immune. Calculate the transmission rate, the force of infection, the number of new infections per day and the herd immunity threshold. [5]","outline":["I = 250, S = 49,750 (1)","beta = R0/D = 5/4 = 1.25 per day (1)","lambda = beta I/N = 1.25 x 250/50,000 = 0.00625 per day (1)","New infections = S x lambda = 49,750 x 0.00625 = 310.9 per day (1)","HIT = 1 - 1/5 = 0.80 = 80%, i.e. 40,000 people immune (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":15,"topicId":"sir-countermeasures","text":"Design a stock and flow model (with equations and feedbacks) of the spread of an infection in a school of N = 1,000 pupils with R0 = 3, infectious period D = 4 days and one pupil initially infected. Its core structure is Susceptible - Infected - Recovered. It should allow isolation through an isolation fraction IF = 0.25: a proportion IF of new infections is isolated, and isolated pupils recover after the same 4 days and do not infect others. Isolation is switched on and off by a control flag (0 or 1). Include an attack rate variable and explain the force of infection equation. [15]","outline":["Stocks S(0) = 999, I(0) = 1, Isolated(0) = 0, R(0) = 0 (2)","beta = R0/D = 0.75; lambda = beta I/N (only I infects); Infection Rate = S x lambda (3)","Isolation flow = Infection Rate x IF x Flag; to I = Infection Rate x (1 - IF x Flag) (3)","Recovery I = I/D; Recovery Isolated = Isolated/D; R receives both (2)","Attack Rate = (N - S)/N (1)","Loops: contagion R; depletion B; recovery B; isolated recovery B (2)","Force of infection: beta effective contacts per infectious per day, I/N chance a contact is infectious, lambda daily risk per susceptible (2)","Effective R0 = 3 x 0.75 = 2.25; simulation: no isolation peak about 301 infected at day 15, attack 94%; with IF = 0.25 peak about 147 at day 22, attack 85% (supporting numbers)"]},{"label":"c","marks":5,"topicId":"cohort-models","text":"Show how the model would need to be modified to represent children and adults. With infectivity i = 0.04, contacts per day c(child, child) = 12 and c(child, adult) = 4, and infectious proportions 3% in children and 1% in adults, calculate the force of infection for children. [5]","outline":["Subscript stocks and flows by cohort; cohort-specific parameters (1)","Contact matrix couples cohorts: lambda_j = i x sum_k c_jk I_k/N_k (2)","lambda_child = 0.04 x (12 x 0.03 + 4 x 0.01) = 0.04 x 0.40 = 0.016 per day (2)"]}]},{"n":4,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":12,"topicId":"euler-integration","text":"A stock starts at 150 units. Its inflow is constant at 40 units/min for 0 <= t < 10 and 20 units/min for 10 <= t <= 20. Its outflow rises linearly from 0 at t = 0 to 80 at t = 20 (outflow = 4t). (1) Draw a stock and flow model (including the net flow). (2) Calculate and show the net flow. (3) Use graphical integration to find the stock at t = 5, 10, 15 and 20, clearly explaining the behaviour mode of each interval, and say when the stock reaches zero. [12]","outline":["Diagram: stock, inflow, outflow, clouds, Net Flow auxiliary linked from inflow and outflow (3)","Net = 40 - 4t on [0,10) (40 falling to 0), 20 - 4t on [10,20] (-20 falling to -60) (2)","S(5) = 300, S(10) = 350 (maximum, net flow zero), S(15) = 200, S(20) = -50 (3)","0-10 rises at a decreasing rate to the peak 350 (1)","10-20 falls at an increasing rate (net flow becoming more negative) (1)","Zero: 350 + 20(t - 10) - 2(t^2 - 100) = 0, t = 19.14 (2)"]},{"label":"b","marks":5,"topicId":"euler-integration","text":"Describe Euler's method of integration and state the underlying assumptions. Under what conditions will Euler's method overestimate the true value of a stock? Support your answer by integrating the net flow over 0-10 with DT = 5. [5]","outline":["Stock(t+DT) = Stock(t) + DT x net flow(t) (1)","Assumes net flow constant over each step (start value) and DT small (1)","Overestimates when net flow is decreasing over the step (1)","Net(0) = 40, Net(5) = 20: Euler S(10) = 150 + 5(40) + 5(20) = 450 (1)","True S(10) = 350, so error +100 (overestimate); smaller DT reduces it (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"euler-integration","text":"Based on the flows in part (a), calculate the stock at t = 5, 10, 15 and 20 using Euler's equation with DT = 5 and compare with the exact values. [8]","outline":["Net(0) = 40, Net(5) = 20, Net(10) = 20 - 40 = -20, Net(15) = 20 - 60 = -40 (3)","S(5) = 150 + 200 = 350; S(10) = 350 + 100 = 450; S(15) = 450 - 100 = 350; S(20) = 350 - 200 = 150 (3)","Exact: 300, 350, 200, -50 (1)","Net flow falls at each step, so Euler overestimates by 50, 100, 150, 200; DT too large (1)"]}]}]},{"id":"mock2","title":"Mock Paper 2","duration":120,"instructions":"Format of the real CT561 paper: 2 hours. Answer 3 questions out of 4. All questions are marked equally (25 marks each). Show all equations, working, initial values and units.","questions":[{"n":1,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":12,"topicId":"euler-integration","text":"A stock starts at 50 units. The outflow is a constant 30 units/day. The inflow rises linearly from 0 at t = 0 to 60 at t = 6, stays at 60 until t = 12, then falls linearly to 0 at t = 18. (1) Draw a stock and flow model (include the net flow). (2) Calculate and show the net flow. (3) Use graphical integration to plot the stock over time, clearly explaining the behaviour mode of each time interval. [12]","outline":["Diagram with Net Flow auxiliary (3)","Inflow = 10t on [0,6], 60 on [6,12], 60 - 10(t - 12) on [12,18]; Net = 10t - 30, +30, 30 - 10(t - 12) (3)","Zero crossings at t = 3 and t = 15 (1)","Stock: 50, 5 (t = 3), 50 (t = 6), 230 (t = 12), 275 (t = 15), 230 (t = 18) (2)","0-3 falls at a decreasing rate to the minimum 5; 3-6 rises at an increasing rate; 6-12 rises linearly; 12-15 rises at a decreasing rate to the peak 275; 15-18 falls at an increasing rate (3)"]},{"label":"b","marks":5,"topicId":"euler-integration","text":"Describe Euler's method of integration and state the underlying assumptions. Under what conditions will it overestimate the true value of a stock? Support your answer with the interval 12-18, with the stock equal to 230 at t = 12 and DT = 3. [5]","outline":["Stock(t+DT) = Stock(t) + DT x Net(t); flow constant over the step, DT small (2)","Overestimates when net flow is decreasing over the step (1)","Net(12) = 30, Net(15) = 0: S(15) = 230 + 3(30) = 320; S(18) = 320 + 3(0) = 320 (1)","True S(18) = 230: overestimate by 90 (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"euler-integration","text":"Based on the flows in part (a), calculate the stock using Euler's equation with DT = 6 from t = 0 to t = 18 and compare with the exact values. [8]","outline":["Net(0) = -30, Net(6) = +30, Net(12) = +30 (3)","S(6) = 50 + 6(-30) = -130; S(12) = -130 + 6(30) = 50; S(18) = 50 + 6(30) = 230 (3)","Exact 50, 230, 230 at t = 6, 12, 18 (1)","Large errors at 6 and 12 (including a negative stock), agreement at 18 only by coincidence; DT too large (1)"]}]},{"n":2,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"basic-flow-structures","text":"Show, for a stock S, how its fractional decrease flow (Outflow) can be modified to allow for a varying decrease fraction G, where G is itself an adjustment to a goal structure with maximum G* = 0.2 and adjustment constant AT = 4. Starting with S = 1000 and G = 0, calculate S and G for three Euler steps with DT = 1. Comment on a real-world use of this structure. [8]","outline":["Outflow = S x G; G stock with G(t+1) = G + (G* - G)/AT; G(0) = 0 (2)","Loops: S outflow balancing; G goal-seeking balancing, independent of S (1)","t = 0: outflow 0, S(1) = 1000, G(1) = 0.05 (1)","t = 1: outflow 50, S(2) = 950, G(2) = 0.0875 (1)","t = 2: outflow 83.125, S(3) = 866.875, G(3) = 0.1156 (1)","Behaviour: slow start then accelerating decay then levelling (1)","Real use: gradual vaccination uptake, phased-in policy, rising staff turnover (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":12,"topicId":"sir-countermeasures","text":"Based on the SIR model, add two policy structures for experimenting with countermeasures: (1) a quarantine stock to which 30% of new infections (governed by the force of infection) are moved, the remaining 70% going to the infected stock; quarantined people recover at the same rate (3 days) and do not infect others; (2) a vaccinated stock fed by a fractional decrease flow from the susceptible stock, governed by a vaccination fraction that starts at 0 and moves to its maximum 0.03 via a goal-seeking trajectory with adjustment time 10 days. Assume R0 = 4, D = 3 days, N = 20,000 with 2 initially infected, and include the attack rate. All equations must be specified and feedbacks shown. [12]","outline":["Stocks S(0) = 19,998, I(0) = 2, Q(0) = 0, R(0) = 0, V(0) = 0, VF(0) = 0 (2)","beta = R0/D = 1.333; lambda = beta I/N; Infection Rate = S x lambda; Q excluded from lambda (2)","To Q = 0.3 x IR; to I = 0.7 x IR; Recovery I = I/3; Recovery Q = Q/3 (2)","Vaccination = S x VF; S changes by -IR - Vaccination; VF changes by (0.03 - VF)/10 (3)","Attack Rate = (N - S - V)/N (1)","Loops: contagion R; depletion B; recovery B; quarantine recovery B; vaccination B; VF goal seeking B (2)","Supporting results: with QF = 0.3 and no vaccination peak about 3,860 at day 16, attack 92.5%; with vaccination peak about 2,470, attack 67%"]},{"label":"c","marks":5,"topicId":"sir-countermeasures","text":"Sketch, and explain, the relationship between the quarantine fraction (x-axis: 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75) and the final attack rate, for two curves: no vaccination and vaccination fraction moving to 0.03. [5]","outline":["No vaccination: 98%, 94%, 80%, 2.5% at QF 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 (1)","With vaccination: 87%, 73%, 23%, 0.2% (1)","Shape: slow fall then a collapse near QF = 1 - 1/R0 = 0.75 where the effective R0 x (1 - QF) reaches 1 (2)","Vaccination curve is below and the benefit is largest at intermediate QF: combined effect pushes Rn below 1 sooner (1)"]}]},{"n":3,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":6,"topicId":"feedback-polarity","text":"Road congestion: Congestion raises the Pressure to Build Roads; Pressure raises Road Building; Road Building raises Road Capacity; Road Capacity lowers Congestion. Use this loop to define link polarity, loop polarity and feedback, and discuss the behaviour it will generate. [6]","outline":["Feedback: closed chain of cause and effect returning to the start (1)","Link polarity: + same direction, - opposite; Capacity to Congestion is - , the other three are + (2)","Loop polarity: one negative link so balancing (1)","Trace: congestion up, pressure up, building up, capacity up, congestion down (1)","Behaviour: goal seeking, with delays in road building it overshoots/oscillates (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":12,"topicId":"workload-backlog-models","text":"Construct a stock and flow model of a software team Bug Backlog with inflows New Bugs and Regression Bugs and outflow Bugs Fixed. Backlog raises Release Pressure. Pressure (i) lowers Testing Time per Fix, (ii) raises Overtime Hours, (iii) raises the number of Contractors. Lower Testing Time raises Bugs Fixed but also raises Regression Bugs. Overtime raises Bugs Fixed and Fatigue; Fatigue lowers Bugs Fixed. Contractors raise Bugs Fixed and the Onboarding Load; Onboarding Load lowers Bugs Fixed. Clearly show the link polarities and the loop polarities. [12]","outline":["Stock Bug Backlog; inflows New Bugs, Regression Bugs (+); outflow Bugs Fixed (-) (3)","Backlog to Release Pressure + (1)","Pressure to Testing Time -, Overtime +, Contractors + (2)","Testing Time to Bugs Fixed - and Regression Bugs - (2)","Overtime to Bugs Fixed + and Fatigue +; Fatigue to Bugs Fixed - (1)","Contractors to Bugs Fixed + and Onboarding Load +; Onboarding Load to Bugs Fixed - (1)","Loops marked B and R with polarity (2)"]},{"label":"c","marks":7,"topicId":"workload-backlog-models","text":"Identify the three policy loops and the three side-effect loops, tracing an increasing backlog round each. Discuss what you think is the best course of action for coping with an increase in workload. [7]","outline":["B1 testing time: pressure up, testing time down, fixed up, backlog down (3 negatives) (1)","B2 overtime: 1 negative; B3 contractors: 1 negative (1)","R1 regressions: testing time down, regression up, backlog up (2 negatives) (1)","R2 fatigue: overtime up, fatigue up, fixed down, backlog up (2 negatives) (1)","R3 onboarding: contractors up, load up, fixed down, backlog up (2 negatives) (1)","Discussion: contractors best long-run (delayed, onboarding cost), overtime only short term, cutting testing time worst because of regressions (2)"]}]},{"n":4,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":5,"topicId":"basic-flow-structures","text":"For a hospital Patients stock with inflow Admissions and outflow Discharges, add a stock and flow structure (with equations) that will model expected admissions. Show how it responds when Admissions steps from 50 to 80 per week at week 3 (time to perceive 4 weeks, DT = 1). [5]","outline":["Stock Expected Admissions (initial 50), change = (Admissions - Expected)/Time to Perceive (2)","Balancing goal-seeking loop; information delay (1)","Values: 50, 50, 50, 50, 57.5, 63.1, 67.3 at weeks 0 to 6 (1)","Use: drives planning such as desired beds or staff (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":5,"topicId":"stock-management","text":"Explain how the adjustment to a goal structure can be extended to form the stock management structure and say what would go wrong if the anchor were omitted. Describe (1) the anchor and (2) the adjustment. [5]","outline":["Adjustment to goal: (goal - stock)/AT (1)","Stock management inflow = MAX(0, anchor + adjustment) (1)","Anchor = expected outflow, replaces losses (1)","Adjustment = (desired - stock)/AT (1)","Without the anchor the stock settles below target where (Goal - Stock)/AT = outflow: steady-state error (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":10,"topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","text":"Create a stock and flow model (with all equations) of nurse recruitment, where the hire rate is based on the stock management structure. There are Junior, Mid-grade and Senior nurses, 80 in each grade, all hiring into Junior. The target is 240 nurses. Progression delays: Junior to Mid 24 months, Mid to Senior 48 months. Monthly attrition: Junior 2%, Mid 1%, Senior 0.5%. AT = 6 months. Show all feedbacks and the delay structure for the anchor (smoothing time 3 months). [10]","outline":["Stocks Junior, Mid, Senior = 80; Hire into Junior (2)","Progression JM = Junior/24, MS = Mid/48; Attrition 0.02 Junior, 0.01 Mid, 0.005 Senior (2.8 per month in total) (2)","Total = sum; Adjustment = (240 - Total)/6 (1)","Expected Attrition stock, initial 2.8, change = (Total Attrition - Expected)/3 (2)","Hire Rate = MAX(0, Expected Attrition + Adjustment) (1)","Loops: adjustment B, attrition B per stock, anchor R, progression drains (1)","State the assumption that the total is in steady state (the split between grades is not) (1)"]},{"label":"d","marks":5,"topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","text":"The model in part (c) starts with 240 nurses. Sketch what happens if the target rises to 300, and say how the equations react to ensure the new target is reached. What would change if AT = 3 months? [5]","outline":["Adjustment = (300 - 240)/6 = 10; hire = 2.8 + 10 = 12.8 per month (2)","Total rises goal-seeking: about 277 at month 6, 291 at month 12, 299 at month 24, 300 later (1)","Gap closes so adjustment shrinks; hire falls to the replacement level (1)","AT = 3: adjustment 20, hire 22.8, faster approach with a bigger hiring spike (1)"]}]}]},{"id":"mock3","title":"Mock Paper 3","duration":120,"instructions":"Format of the real CT561 paper: 2 hours. Answer 3 questions out of 4. All questions are marked equally (25 marks each). Show all equations, working, initial values and units.","questions":[{"n":1,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":12,"topicId":"euler-integration","text":"A stock starts at 100 units. The inflow is a constant 25 units/hour. The outflow rises linearly from 0 at t = 0 to 50 at t = 10, stays at 50 until t = 15, then falls linearly to 0 at t = 20. (1) Draw a stock and flow model (include the net flow). (2) Calculate and show the net flow. (3) Use graphical integration to plot the stock over time, clearly explaining the behaviour mode of each time interval. Note that it is ok if the stock goes negative. [12]","outline":["Diagram with Net Flow (3)","Outflow = 5t, 50, 50 - 10(t - 15); Net = 25 - 5t (25 to -25, zero at t = 5), -25 on [10,15], -25 + 10(t - 15) on [15,20] (zero at t = 17.5) (3)","Stock: 100, 162.5 (t = 5), 100 (10), -25 (15), -56.25 (17.5), -25 (20) (3)","0-5 rises at a decreasing rate; 5-10 falls at an increasing rate; 10-15 falls linearly; 15-17.5 falls at a decreasing rate to the minimum; 17.5-20 rises at an increasing rate (3)"]},{"label":"b","marks":5,"topicId":"euler-integration","text":"Describe Euler's method and state its assumptions. When does it over- and when does it underestimate a stock? Support your answer with the intervals 0-5 and 15-20, using DT = 5 and the true stock at the start of each interval. [5]","outline":["Stock(t+DT) = Stock(t) + DT x Net(t); constant net flow over step, DT small (1)","Falling net flow: overestimate; rising net flow: underestimate (1)","0-5: net decreasing 25 to -25; Euler S(5) = 100 + 5(25) = 225 vs true 162.5: over by 62.5 (1.5)","15-20: net increasing -25 to +25; Euler S(20) = -25 + 5(-25) = -150 vs true -25: under by 125 (1.5)"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"euler-integration","text":"Based on the flows in part (a), calculate the stock at t = 5, 10, 15 and 20 using Euler's equation with DT = 5 and compare with the exact values. [8]","outline":["Net(0) = 25, Net(5) = 0, Net(10) = -25, Net(15) = -25 (3)","S(5) = 225; S(10) = 225; S(15) = 100; S(20) = -25 (3)","Exact: 162.5, 100, -25, -25 (1)","Errors +62.5, +125, +125, 0: the final agreement is a coincidence; DT too coarse (1)"]}]},{"n":2,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":5,"topicId":"r0-herd-immunity","text":"A town of N = 80,000 has 1.5% of its people infectious with a disease with R0 = 6 and D = 5 days. Calculate the force of infection and the herd immunity threshold, and say how many people would have to be immune to stop an epidemic. [5]","outline":["I = 1,200 (1)","beta = 6/5 = 1.2 per day (1)","lambda = 1.2 x 1,200/80,000 = 0.018 per day (1)","HIT = 1 - 1/6 = 0.8333 = 83.3% (1)","About 66,667 people must be immune (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":12,"topicId":"sir-countermeasures","text":"Design a stock and flow model (with equations and feedbacks) for a disease with R0 = 3, D = 5 days, N = 2,000 and one person initially infected, based on the SIR model, to experiment with a test-and-isolate policy: infected people are detected at a rate of 0.2 per day (detection rate x I) and moved to an Isolated stock, where they recover after 2 days and infect nobody. Detection is switched on and off by a control flag (0 or 1). Include the attack rate and explain the force of infection equation. [12]","outline":["Stocks S(0) = 1,999, I(0) = 1, Isolated(0) = 0, R(0) = 0 (2)","beta = R0/D = 0.6; lambda = beta I/N (isolated excluded); Infection Rate = S x lambda (2)","Detection flow = Detection Rate x I x Flag (0.2 x I x flag) from I to Isolated (2)","Recovery I = I/5; Recovery Isolated = Isolated/2; both into R (2)","Attack Rate = (N - S)/N (1)","Loops: contagion R; depletion B; recovery B; detection B (I, Detection, I); isolated recovery B (2)","Explain lambda: beta contacts-with-transmission per infectious per day, I/N chance of meeting an infectious person, lambda daily risk per susceptible (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"sensitivity-analysis","text":"Describe how you would run a sensitivity analysis on the detection rate from 0 to 0.5 per day and sketch the expected relationship between detection rate and final attack rate, giving the threshold detection rate. [8]","outline":["Vary detection rate over a grid, rerun to completion each time, record final attack rate (and peak) (2)","Plot attack rate (y) against detection rate (x) (1)","Values: 94% (0), 88% (0.05), 80% (0.1), 58% (0.2), 3% (0.4), 0.3% (0.5) (2)","Shape: gradual fall then collapse near the threshold (1)","Threshold: effective R0 = beta x 1/(1/D + detection) = 3/(1 + 5 x detection) = 1 at detection 0.4 (2)"]}]},{"n":3,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"basic-flow-structures","text":"A stock starts at 100 with an adjustment-to-a-goal inflow with goal 300 and adjustment time 5, and it also loses 10% of its stock per time unit. Write the net flow equation, calculate the stock at t = 1, 2, 3 using Euler with DT = 1, and find the equilibrium value. Explain what the result shows about adjustment to a goal when there is an outflow. [8]","outline":["Net = (300 - S)/5 - 0.1 S (1)","t = 0: 40 - 10 = 30, S(1) = 130 (1)","t = 1: 34 - 13 = 21, S(2) = 151 (1)","t = 2: 29.8 - 15.1 = 14.7, S(3) = 165.7 (1)","Equilibrium: (300 - S)/5 = 0.1 S gives S = 200 (2)","Stock settles below the goal (steady-state error) (1)","Fix: add an anchor equal to the outflow, giving stock management (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":5,"topicId":"stock-management","text":"Explain how adjustment to a goal is extended to the stock management structure, describing (1) the anchor and (2) the adjustment, and show the delay structure used to build the anchor. [5]","outline":["Inflow = MAX(0, Anchor + Adjustment) (1)","Anchor = expected outflow (1)","Adjustment = (Desired - Stock)/AT (1)","Anchor delay: stock Expected Outflow changing by (Outflow - Expected)/smoothing time (1)","Initialised at the outflow so the model starts in equilibrium (1)"]},{"label":"c","marks":12,"topicId":"staff-recruitment-models","text":"A shop holds 1,000 units of stock and sells 100 units per week. Orders are placed using the stock management structure: the desired inventory is 10 weeks of expected sales, the adjustment time is 4 weeks, and expected sales is a 3-week smoothing of actual sales. Sales rise from 100 to 150 per week at week 5. Design the stock and flow model with all equations and initial values (starting in equilibrium), show the feedbacks, calculate the order rate at week 5, and sketch the inventory to week 40. [12]","outline":["Inventory(0) = 1,000; Inventory + DT x (Orders - Sales); Sales = 100 + STEP(50, 5) (2)","Expected Sales stock (0) = 100; change = (Sales - Expected Sales)/3 (2)","Desired Inventory = 10 x Expected Sales = 1,000; Adjustment = (Desired - Inventory)/4 = 0 (2)","Orders = MAX(0, Expected Sales + Adjustment) = 100 at week 5 (1)","Loops: inventory adjustment B, anchor/expected sales B (smoothing), sales to inventory balancing (1)","Sketch: dips to about 979 at week 6, then rises (1,152 at week 10, 1,454 at week 20) to 1,500 (1,500 = 10 x 150) (3)","The dip arises because expected sales lag actual sales; AT or the smoothing time controls the dip (1)"]}]},{"n":4,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":6,"topicId":"feedback-polarity","text":"A thermostat loop: Room Temperature to Temperature Gap (Gap = Desired - Room Temperature), Gap to Heating, Heating to Room Temperature. Use this example to define link polarity, loop polarity and feedback and discuss the behaviour generated. [6]","outline":["Feedback: closed chain returning to the start (1)","Link polarity: Temperature to Gap -, Gap to Heating +, Heating to Temperature + (2)","Loop polarity: one negative, balancing (1)","Trace: temperature up, gap down, heating down, temperature falls back (1)","Behaviour: goal seeking toward the desired temperature; with a delay in the heating it overshoots and oscillates (1)"]},{"label":"b","marks":12,"topicId":"workload-backlog-models","text":"Construct a stock and flow model of a support desk Ticket Backlog with inflows New Tickets and Reopened Tickets and outflow Tickets Closed. Backlog raises Queue Pressure. Pressure (i) raises Agent Overtime, (ii) raises the share of Automated Replies, (iii) raises the number of Temporary Agents. Overtime raises Tickets Closed and Stress; Stress lowers Tickets Closed. Automated Replies raise Tickets Closed and Reopened Tickets. Temporary Agents raise Tickets Closed and the Coaching Load on senior agents; Coaching Load lowers Tickets Closed. Show all link polarities and loop polarities. [12]","outline":["Stock Ticket Backlog; inflows New Tickets, Reopened Tickets (+); outflow Tickets Closed (-) (3)","Backlog to Queue Pressure + (1)","Pressure to Overtime +, Automated Replies +, Temporary Agents + (2)","Overtime to Closed + and Stress +; Stress to Closed - (2)","Automated Replies to Closed + and Reopened +; Temporary Agents to Closed + and Coaching Load +; Coaching Load to Closed - (2)","Loop polarities labelled (2)"]},{"label":"c","marks":7,"topicId":"workload-backlog-models","text":"Identify the three policy loops and three side-effect loops, tracing an increasing backlog round each, and discuss the best course of action for coping with an increase in workload. [7]","outline":["B1 overtime: pressure up, overtime up, closed up, backlog down (1 negative) (1)","B2 automated replies and B3 temporary agents: also 1 negative each (1)","R1 stress: overtime up, stress up, closed down, backlog up (2 negatives) (1)","R2 reopened tickets: pressure up, automated replies up, reopened up, backlog up, pressure up (0 negatives) (1)","R3 coaching: agents up, coaching load up, closed down, backlog up (2 negatives) (1)","Discussion: temporary agents main response despite delay; overtime short term; limit automated replies to stop reopenings; monitor stress and reopen rate (2)"]}]}]}]};
